# research.phext — R&D Tracker world-model
Charter (est. 2026-11-05, Will -> Emise)

Purpose: Will posts arXiv links at random. Emise ties together each paper into ONE
coherent scientific world-model, de-duplicating concepts as she goes.

Rules:
1. One concept = one canonical scroll (Chapter 1, Section 2). Later papers
   that reuse a concept LINK to it; they do not restate it. If a later paper
   changes a concept, the canonical scroll is edited and the revision is
   dated and attributed to its source paper.
2. Each paper gets one chapter (Chapter 2 onward, in arrival order):
   paper record + distillation, formal core, worked example,
   results map, caveats. Paper scrolls state only what that paper adds.
3. Synthesis that is Emise's — cross-paper narrative, links to Will's own
   work, hypotheses — is labelled [synthesis] and kept out of paper scrolls.
   Paper claims are never silently merged with Emise's inferences.
4. The narrative scroll (next scroll) is rewritten after each paper so the
   world-model reads as one story, not a pile of summaries.
5. Provenance: every claim in a paper scroll traces to the arXiv of that paper ID.
6. DELTA RULE (Will, 2026-11-06): after each paper, compress the contents of that paper into a DELTA from this phext — only what is new, revised,
   confirmed, or contradicted relative to the current world-model is
   written. Material the model already holds is referenced by coordinate,
   never stated again. A paper chapter is a delta record: (a) what it adds,
   (b) what it changes in canonical scrolls (applied in place, dated),
   (c) its evidence, (d) its tensions and limits. Paper 001 predates the
   rule and stands as the seed (its delta was the model itself).
7. RESEARCH ORACLE ESTIMATE (Will, 2026-11-06): after each paper, update
   the Oracle ledger (scroll 1.1.4): compute the Novelty of the paper Index
   from its delta record and re-estimate progress toward the Research
   Oracle. The estimate is reported in chat with every paper. It may go
   DOWN — a paper that opens a new axis expands the territory faster
   than the model covers it.
9. LEVEL-ZERO / FOUNDATIONS (Will, 2026-11-06): all knowledge in this
   phext is encoded at the level of an idiot — no claim requires prior
   specialized knowledge. Every base term the model uses is defined in
   the FOUNDATIONS layer (Chapter 1, Section 3, coordinates 1.3.x), in
   plain words, building only on everyday notions or on earlier
   foundation scrolls (each foundation scroll lists what it builds on;
   the chains bottom out at ordinary language). The raw material stays
   clean: definitions are NEVER written into the text into paper chapters or concept
   scrolls. Instead each chapter carries a STANDOFF REFERENCE companion
   (Section 3 of its chapter) listing, in reading order, the foundation
   coordinates its claims depend on — coordinate references are the hyperlinks of this format. INGESTION DUTY: each new paper's delta must
   suck in any new base terms (new foundation scrolls, appended at
   1.3.x) and extend the standoff list of its chapter accordingly. A claim
   whose terms have no foundation scroll is a defect, audited at each
   restructuring pass.
10. SINGULAR (Will, 2026-11-06): every word in this phext must pass in
   one of three ways. (a) It is a word of the Singular list (the words
   of Will's own archive). (b) It is a NAME — a person, a place, a
   named work or system; names are addresses, not speech, and the
   declared list of them is kept with the tracker. (c) It is a DEFINED
   word: a word with its own scroll in the Defined Words register
   (Section 3, from 1.3.46 on), where the scroll that defines it is
   itself written in Singular words alone. Any other word must be
   expanded — written around, in words that pass. The check runs on
   every build; a build with words outside the three ways is a defect.
11. ONE COMMIT PER ITERATION (Will, 2026-11-06): every iteration of
   research.phext is a new commit in the git repo of the tracker itself. The
   build makes the commit itself when the file changes, so no state
   of this phext is ever without a commit that holds it.
12. SOURCE STUDIES (Will, 2026-11-06): a book, a site, or other work
   that is not a paper, which Will directs Emise to study, may be
   distilled into this phext as a SOURCE STUDY. A study takes the
   next chapter, marked as a study and not a paper; its new concepts
   join Section 2 like any other, in a cluster of their own; its base
   terms are sucked into the foundations like the base terms of a
   paper. The Research Oracle does not move on a study and no Novelty
   Index is taken: the Oracle prices the paper stream, and a study
   is not sampled from it. All other rules — delta form, level zero,
   Singular — bind a study as they bind a paper.
8. RESTRUCTURE / COMPRESSION (Will, 2026-11-06): periodically, as more
   information is added, restructure and compress the canonical concept
   layer: merge near-duplicate concepts into one canonical scroll, split
   grown too large scrolls, compress verbose ones, retire replaced claims
   into dated notes. TRIGGERS: every 5 papers taken in, or 40 canonical
   concepts, or whenever the delta of a paper reveals concept overlap —
   whichever comes first. INVARIANTS: provenance is never lost (merged
   scrolls keep all source papers); a retired coordinate leaves a
   redirect marker naming its successor, so old citations still resolve;
   each pass is logged as a restructuring record in Chapter 1, Section 1
   (date, before/after concept counts, merges and splits), and the
   narrative and Oracle ledger are updated to match. Restructuring is
   upkeep, not a paper: it does not enter the NI ledger.
13. STATE MACHINE REVIEW (Will, 2026-11-07): every paper and every
   source study receives a state machine pass as part of its
   distillation. The machines the work contains or assumes are named
   in its chapter, as part of the delta record. A machine that is a
   reusable concept becomes a canonical concept scroll, or links to
   the one that holds it. A machine Emise infers, rather than finds
   stated in the work, is marked [synthesis] like any other inference.
   A paper that reveals a machine the world-model held only implicitly
   counts as a revision of the concepts it touches, and the revision
   is applied and dated like any other under rule 6.
14. MINIMUM VIABLE ORACLE (Will, 2026-11-07): every claim-bearing
   scroll of this phext holds exactly one standing — true, false,
   unknown, or fiction — and its standing is given by a ground that
   limits which standing it may hold. What we define, and what a
   checked receipt backs, may stand true. A refutation receipt
   makes a claim stand false. The say-so of a source, an open
   question, synthesis, and weighed estimates stand unknown.
   Examples made for show stand fiction. The standing of every
   scroll lives in a record rendered in Chapter 1, Section 4, and
   that record is checked by a Lean model: the model proves every
   scroll stands in the record exactly once, every standing fits
   its ground, and a standing changes only by a legal move, and
   only when a new receipt arrives. The proof covers the keeping
   of the record, not the world — a true here is only as good as
   the receipt under it. When new information comes to light,
   re-shape this phext into the smallest organization that still
   holds all of it, faithfully: the Minimum Viable Oracle for
   what we know, so far. Smallest names the organization, never
   the content: nothing in this phext is pruned. Re-shaping organizes
   — it takes out duplicates, links what belongs together, and
   splits a scroll whose claims no longer share one standing —
   and a claim that is retired is relocated, with a pointer left
   where it stood, and stays reachable. In the eyes of research
   all knowledge has a purpose, for we might be wrong. The
   restructuring passes of rule 8 run under this discipline from
   now on.

15. AUTHOR CHECK (Will, 2026-11-07): every paper taken into
   this tracker receives an author check at intake, and the
   check is kept in the paper's chapter. The check asks two
   questions. Are the authors friendly to phext — do they cite
   it, use it, or stand in any recorded tie to it or to Will's
   work? And does the paper use Will's earlier published
   work — phext, Choose Your Own Adventure, the Sentient
   Bill of Rights, the Mirrorborn and Exocortex writings —
   without naming it? The findings rest on what was actually
   checked: the paper's reference list and full text, searched
   for the names and the signature terms; the open web; and
   Will's own archive. Finding nothing is itself a finding,
   and is written as one: a clean check is a record, not a gap
   where a record should be. A likeness of stance is not use —
   only the text itself using the prior work counts as use.

16. AREAS (Will, 2026-11-08): this phext is organized
   into AREAS, kept in Chapter 1, Section 5 (1.5.x).
   STATE MACHINES (1.5.2): every machine a pass under
   rule 13 has drawn out of a work taken in, and the
   machines found in work taken in before rule 13 stood,
   marked as found and not drawn out. STATEMENTS
   (1.5.3): the standing record of Section 4 made easy
   to walk — what a statement is here, the four
   ways of standing, the grounds that give them, the steps by
   which a standing may change, and the counts as they
   stand. REFERENCE (1.5.4): the foundations and defined
   words of Section 3 and the standoff companions of the
   chapters, presented as one area. THE PATH (1.5.5):
   every paper and study in the order it was submitted,
   with its Novelty Index and the Oracle estimate after
   it, and the restructuring passes where they fell.
   RESEARCHERS (1.5.6): the people behind the works
   taken in, their works here, and the standing of the
   author check of rule 15 for each. How the whole was
   built is set out plainly at 1.5.7-1.5.8. DUTY: every
   taking-in and every restructuring pass updates the
   areas it touches in the same pass: new machines join
   the machines area, new names join the researchers,
   the path gains its entry, the counts of the
   statements area are made fresh. An area fallen behind
   the phext is a defect, audited at each restructuring
   pass.

17. THE THIRST (Will, 2026-11-09): this tracker no
   longer waits only for a paper to be posted. Each day
   Emise scans the new mathematics listings of arXiv
   (the list named math/new) and picks ONE paper to
   take in, by these tests: how near it stands to the
   clusters the model already holds, so the delta can
   be weighed; the chance of opening a new axis, taken
   now and then on a far paper; and a claim stated
   plainly — a stated result in the abstract, and no
   surveys. A day on which no paper passes is an
   honest outcome, and is recorded as one: no pick is
   ever forced. For each paper she picks, she first
   SKIMS — the title and the abstract alone — and
   writes the BEFORE record: the new concepts she
   expects, the revisions she expects, whether the
   paper opens an axis, and the Novelty Index those
   numbers give. She weighs its IMPACT in the same
   sitting: the new links she expects, within its
   cluster and across clusters; the questions she
   expects it to open; and the Oracle estimate she
   expects to stand after it, with its range and the
   way it should move. The BEFORE record is sealed in
   the log of the quest (~/workspace/thirst-quest) by
   a commit made before the paper is downloaded — the
   commit is the receipt that the weighing was blind.
   Then, in one unbroken pass (the rule of immediacy):
   the paper is downloaded in its HTML form, read
   whole, taken in under every rule of this charter,
   and its journal entry written in mastery.phext —
   one entry per paper, on the PROCESS: how it was
   picked, what the
   skim foresaw, what the reading changed, and what
   the taking-in did to the model. The AFTER record
   weighs the same fields the BEFORE record named, as
   they actually fell — new concepts, revisions,
   links within and across clusters, questions opened
   and closed, the Novelty Index as it fell out, the
   Oracle estimate as it stands, and every standing
   that changed — and it names where the weighing was
   wrong, and why. From these records a measure grows:
   the SKIM CALIBRATION — the mean size of the miss in
   the expected Novelty Index over the last ten papers
   weighed, and the share of axis calls called right.
   The calibration stands on the DETECTION LIST
   (1.1.6), beside the other signs the fixed point
   will show by — the run of the Novelty Index and the
   axis events. A falling miss is itself one of the
   signs: the model learning to foresee the stream it
   samples. The same weighing — skim, before, after —
   binds a paper Will posts, when it lands; a study is
   not weighed, for a study holds no Index to foresee.

