# World-model narrative — v16 (after Paper 011, 2026-11-09)

The economically relevant unit of intelligence is not the isolated agent
but the organization. A lone benchmark score says how well one agent does
alone; a market tests how capabilities COMBINE and SCALE inside firms that
compete in equilibrium. That is the founding claim of this world-model,
from Paper 001 (Imas, Koh & Legg, 'Organizing Intelligence').

The story so far, in one arc:
Capabilities are multidimensional and jagged. What a firm produces depends
on two different things that benchmarks conflate: competence (how good its
members are at tasks — a level shift) and coordination (how well members
work together — which sets how productivity scales with firm size).

Humans and AI differ on exactly two structural axes. (a) Supply: human
expertise is fixed, so scarce talent earns Ricardian rents; AI expertise is
reproducible at the cost of compute, so its rents are competed away.
(b) Coordination limits: humans hit a span-of-control ceiling (mean
coordination < 1 in the scale of the model factor), which caps firm size — the
Coasean ceiling. AI might not.

From those two differences everything else follows. Because human skills
are jagged in different directions, sorting complementary humans into one
firm creates mosaic intelligence: the team reaches capability combinations
no member holds alone, so human firms can beat AI that is better than the
average human on every standalone test. Rising AI competence therefore
need not transform the economy: it shrinks firms, makes less tall hierarchies,
and raises the value of human coordinator profiles first.

The transformation, when it comes, is a coordination event, not a
competence event. If AI coordination crosses the supercooperation
threshold, returns to scale flip sign, the Coasean ceiling breaks, and
market structure phase-transitions to large, concentrated, AI-heavy firms.
Displacement then changes character: step by step inside mixed firms while the
composition envelope is concave, sudden across firms — whole human firms
wiped out at a threshold — when it turns convex. And wages split into a
technology effect (what AI does to the value of a human inside a fixed firm)
and a re-organization effect (what happens when the firm itself changes the size of and
re-mixes), which can reverse the first.

Paper 002 (Krentsel et al., 'Exo') rotates the axis from space to time.
Paper 001 holds capability profiles fixed and lets an outside world
re-sort them; Paper 002 asks what a single agent's capability even is,
given that the agent can rewrite the machinery that expresses it. Between
the model weights and the outcome sits the harness — context assembly,
tools, memory retrieval, pressing together — and that layer is CODE: open to looking inside,
editable, by the agent itself, mid-task. Collapse the external optimizer
into the worker and improvement becomes endogenous: the agent pays a
specialization cost early (building tools, revising context policy) and
spreads the cost of it over the rest of the task and across future tasks, at fixed
weights. What makes this that can be lived through is an old operating-systems split:
a minimal protected mechanism layer (append-only history, a guardian
that alone can activate a new policy, independent rollback) around an
everything-else-is-editable policy layer — including the policy for how
to improve.

Paper 003 (Russell et al., 'StoryScope') turns from the producers to the
product. Hold the prompt constant, let a human and five LLMs each write
the story, and induce a feature space from the differences: 304 discourse-
level decisions per story — agency, time structure, revelation, thematic
being stated out loud — deliberately ignoring surface style. The geometry that
falls out is the first of the model empirical picture of a capability envelope:
the five AI systems, from five different labs, cluster in one shared
region of narrative space, while human stories sit outside it, more
dispersed and measurably more rare in their combinations of choices. AI
fiction over-explains its themes, resolves in a tidy way along a single causal
track, and renders emotion through the body; human fiction jumps in time,
subverts its own causality, names its influences, and leaves its morals
ambiguous. Each model still carries a fingerprint — Claude flat and
full of respect, GPT full of gossip, Kimi the generic center — but the fingerprints are
variations inside a cluster, not escapes from it.

Paper 004 (Li et al., 'Hierarchical Mixture of Experts') drops to the
level of a single model's internals — and finds Paper 001's organization
chart already there. The task is unglamorous and expensive: predict the performance of an FPGA from a C++ kernel plus its synthesis pragma instructions, well
enough to search pragma designs without running hours-long synthesis —
and to keep predicting well on kernels never seen in training, where
single models fail from domain shift. The fix is structural: replace
monolithic graph network blocks with mixtures of experts at three granularities
of the program graph (node, basic block, whole graph), then a second,
high-level mixture that learns how much to trust each granularity per
input. The experts specialize on their own — by pragma type, by
substructure — and the gating networks route each new input to the
experts whose regions it resembles, so knowledge transfers to unseen
kernels through shared substructures rather than through forced
representational being the same. The failure mode is organizational too:
left alone, gating collapses onto the single fastest-improving expert
(expert polarization), the mosaic dies, and generalization dies with
it; a two-stage training regime exists precisely to keep the division
of labor alive.

Paper 005 (Akhmedov, 'Braided Multisections and Akhmedov's Fake
Quadrics') leaves the continent. It is pure mathematics: closed
symplectic 4-manifolds that have exactly the rational cohomology ring
of S^2 x S^2 yet are not S^2 x S^2 — an infinite family of impostors,
built by gluing complements of braided multisections in ruled
surfaces, and told apart only by integral invariants: the torsion in
their first homology, which rational measurement deletes entirely.
the work of the paper is the honest kind of explicit: handlebody
recipes in parts of the pieces, chain systems computed, torsion given
a geometric face, and gluing maps specified as actual marked surface
diffeomorphisms — because, as the author insists, the matrices those
maps induce on homology do not by themselves determine what you get
when you glue. The sums come out aspherical and symplectically
minimal, and rationally identical to the standard quadric they are
faking.

The weave, so far: Paper 001 says intelligence is ORGANIZED — across
agents, by coordination. Paper 002 says intelligence Organizes again ITSELF —
across time, by self-editing policy under protected mechanism. Paper 003
says that, for now, intelligence CONVERGES — across producers, toward a
shared, more narrow region of what gets made. Paper 004 says intelligence
is organized at EVERY scale it has been looked at: the firm (P001), the own of the agent policy stack (P002), the population of producers (P003),
and now the inside of a single network, where learned experts hold
jagged, complementary profiles, a gating coordinator routes work among
them, and the known of the system failure mode is the collapse of that
division of labor. The leverage keeps sitting outside raw competence:
in how capability is combined, expressed, distributed — and routed.
The gates come back too: a coordination threshold (P001), an intelligence
floor (P002), a convergence pull (P003), and a polarization collapse
that must be actively trained against (P004).

Open frontier: (1) Is real multi-agent coordination approaching the
supercooperation threshold? Paper 001's estimate says current AI teams
sit at gamma in [0.48, 0.68], below the line, though speed gains are
not counted. (2) Does runtime self-improvement move an agent SYSTEM's
effective coordination — is a self-editing harness a coordination
technology for a population of copies? (3) The observed default at
output level is convergence — so what, if anything, makes a population
of AI systems DIVERGE to good effect rather than collapse toward the
modal artifact? Paper 004 offers the first mechanism-shaped hint:
inside an MoE, divergence survives only under active maintenance
(two-stage training, load-balancing pressure) against a constant
collapse gradient — divergence is a maintained state, not a default.
(4) New with Paper 005, the frontier gets wider past AI entirely: the
stream can jump domains without warning, and the second of the model
continent is currently joined to the first only by method-level
echoes that ring — measure at more fine granularity than the summary you are
handed (the quadrics are rational twins and integral strangers), and
never mistake a matrix of the gluing for the gluing. Whether those
echoes that ring are deep or merely rhyming is itself an open question,
and the tracker records it as one (Q16) rather than deciding it. (5) New with Paper 006: the territory gains a bridge — the
developer's specification of structure is a control surface
over agents, priced in tests — and the open edge is whether
that pricing survives contact with real prompted agents,
whose spread does not condition the way the theorems suppose
(Q20), and who should hold the skeleton when the agent edits
its own harness (Q23). (6) New with Paper 007: the attention layer itself — whether the
sublinear shape of the data, measured to 256K, holds at the
scales the method points at (Q24), and whether the share
that depth taxes can be governed from the measurements of
the run itself (Q25).

Paper 006 (Golatkar et al., 'Skeleton Coding') turns to the
one seat the model had not yet sat in: the developer's.
Papers 001-005 and the studies look at agents, their outputs,
what is inside them, and the checkers over their claims; this paper
asks what the human on the other side should hand the agent
in the first place. Its answer: structure. An agent first
chooses a structure — data types, parts, boundaries,
signatures — and that choice fixes the class of programs it
can still produce; tests then only choose inside the class.
So the number of tests a developer must write is not a
property of the task but of a structure the agent chose and
the developer cannot see: a test set that pins one structure down
can leave another wide open, the same test can be worth a
full bit or almost nothing, and some requirements — how the
code is built, not what it returns — no output test can see
at all. Give the structure yourself, as a skeleton of
signatures, contracts and invariants, and the cost of
steering falls, in the paper's cases by orders of magnitude;
make the contracts able to be checked and the loop closes, part by
part, at a cost that adds in place of multiplying. The weave
tightens: this is Paper 002's harness seen from the other
side of the glass — in the harness, structure is settled by
the agent's own rewrites; the skeleton is that same settled
structure, handed over by the developer from outside — and
it is the studies' checker pattern
promoted to a design method: contracts small enough to check,
parts independent enough to check separately (1.2.57-1.2.64).
The measured shadow: code written under behavioral
specification alone erodes faster — complexity piling into
the hardest functions — while code written under a standing
skeleton erodes slower, because the skeleton records which
decisions are requirements and which behavior is accident.

Paper 007 opens the layer underneath all of it. Every system
above runs on attention: a weighing of the past, kept in a cache
that costs the square of its length to search. The paper measures
two facts — one query needs a sublinear slice of that past, yet
every part of it is needed by some query to come — and builds the
only shape both facts allow: keep everything, and keep an index
of it. A tree of summaries over the cache, routed by mass,
made deeper as the cache grows, distilled into models that were
never trained for it. For this tracker the paper does double
duty: it is the first account here of the machinery the other
systems are made of, and a worked example of the bet of this tracker itself — that an explicit, built address structure, honestly kept,
beats a cleverer opaque one. The bet is not settled by the paper:
past 256K tokens its numbers are the output of a reckoning, and
the schedule that would keep the tree honest at depth is named,
not built (Q25).

Added by Study S001 (a book, not a paper): a third continent joins —
CHECKED claims. MiniLean4 (Study S001, Chapter 7) builds the machine
the other continents keep assuming: a checker small enough to trust,
in which a proof is a program and a claim is a type (1.2.40-1.2.47).
It touches Paper 002 at one real point — the kernel is the guardian
pattern (1.2.19) with the guard moved from state to claims — and
Paper 005 at the level of taste: both are about telling apart things
that look the same at coarse measure (the two equalities of 1.2.43
are the quadric lesson, told in logic). The tracker holds the honesty
of the study as part of the model: its guarantee is proved for the
core system only, and says so (1.2.47).

Added by Studies S002 and S003 (tools, not papers): the continent
of checked claims gains its road and its first city. Aeneas
(Chapter 8) is the road: Rust programs carried into the kernels of
cluster F as plain functions, so the shipped code itself — not a
model of it — is what gets proved (1.2.48-1.2.52). h5i (Chapter 9)
is the city: the author of MiniLean4 running the same pattern at
the scale of a working system — a web framework whose small kernel
is carried into Lean by Aeneas itself and proved there, wrapped in
an untrusted shell, beside tools that write the receipt before the
act (1.2.53-1.2.56). The three studies stand in one line: the
kernel (S001), the road into the kernel (S002), and the pattern
lived at scale (S003). What the line adds up to, as a claim of
this world-model: trust is an architecture. It is not a feeling
about code; it is a decision about which small part must be right,
with everything else made a mere producer of things that part
checks.

Added by Study S004 (an article of 1996, not a paper): the
proving continent gains the ground it stands on. Thirty
years before the tracker, the article of Chapter 12 set down
the hazard in its first full form: on the stack, a program's
data and its control lie side by side in one region that can
be written; a copy that checks no length can run from the
one into the other; and a return address written over is a program redirected (1.2.73-1.2.80). Nothing in the papers is
changed by the study, and the Oracle does not move. What the
study adds to the story is the reason the third continent
exists: the kernel of MiniLean4, the road of Aeneas, and the
lived pattern of h5i are the long answer to a short 1996
lesson — that a write which should have been only data
became direction, because no part of the machine was charged
with keeping the two apart. The of the tracker checked claims now
have their unchecked ancestor on the record.

Added by Study S005 (a living code base, not a paper): the
story gains a workshop. Until now the model held claims,
the machines inside claims, and the keeping of claims;
Astryx (Chapter 13) is a system that holds its own keeping
in the open — a design system whose parts carry their
documents as data, whose conventions are rules a tool can
check, whose records say which of them govern, and whose
worth is measured by what a fresh agent can build with it
(1.2.81-1.2.88). It stands nearest to Paper 006: the
skeleton of that paper, built at the scale of a company —
structure written down so that work can be checked part
by part, by people and by agents on one surface. And it
stands near this tracker itself: two systems that keep
each claim at one standing, built on opposite sides of
the same question — theirs change on approval, ours only
on receipts (Q34). The Oracle does not move; a study is
not a sample of the paper stream. What moves is the map:
the model now holds a worked example of the thing Paper
006 priced in the abstract — a structure an agent can be
checked against, shipped and in daily use.

Added by Study S006 (a course, not a paper): the
story gains its ground floor. Until now the model
held the systems the papers describe — firms of
agents, harnesses, mixtures of experts, attention —
in the language the papers speak, which is numbers.
TinyTorch (Chapter 14) is the layer under that
language, built by hand: the tensor the numbers
live in, the tape that finds their slopes, the loop
that turns slopes into learning, and the small hot
kernels where the time goes (1.2.89-1.2.97). It is
also the first source here whose subject is learning
itself: a course that measures its students by what
they can rebuild — the landmarks of the history
of the field, re-made with their own code — and keeps
their standing in a ledger a gate of checks writes.
The Oracle does not move; a study is not a sample of
the paper stream. What moves is the depth of the
map: the model's claims about machines now stand on
a layer where the machines are small enough to be
made by one pair of hands, in the open, with the
answers printed — and the tracker records, as a
question (Q38), whether its own keeping could stand
the same light.

Added by Paper 008 (2026-11-09): the story gains the
second subject of the annex. The first symplectic paper
(Paper 005) asked when two manifolds are the same;
this one asks what maps a manifold must allow, and
finds the sharp claim of that asking — the strong
Arnold conjecture, that no Hamiltonian map can stand
on fewer fixed points than a function's fewest
resting points — already fallen, twice, before the
paper arrived, and fallen on this paper's ground by
maps its authors build one film-stretch at a time
(1.2.98-1.2.103). The model's deepest count now
crosses a hundred concepts without leaving cluster E:
the taking-in was small because the ground was held,
and the Oracle rises on a taking-in that small. And
it is the first paper the tracker chose for itself —
weighed blind under the Thirst, its delta seen
nearly whole before it was read — so the story now
includes the tracker reading its own forecast
against the paper, in the open, and scoring where
the forecast was wrong.

Added by Paper 009 (2026-11-09): the stream leaves the
workshop and the library for the sky — and the annex gains
its second wing. A particle falls, or does not, toward the
simplest heavy thing that is not a point: a segment
carrying any density at all. The paper sorts the endings
of its fall completely — every breakdown an approach,
every approach flat, every flat approach a meeting — and
hands the navigator a cone to test a course by, with a
clock. The wing hangs from the first by one stone: the
Hamiltonian flow, whose made maps the Arnold wing counts
and whose endings this wing classifies. It is the second paper of the Thirst,
and the first Will posted into the stream
himself: the hunt is now two hunters wide, and the weighing
held — six concepts foretold, six arrived; the revision
foretold never came, the axis foretold opened.

Added by Paper 010 (2026-11-09): the map gains the
mathematics it itself runs on. Will asked which area of
mathematics the model needed; the census of the tracker
answered probability and statistics — the Oracle is an
estimate, the skim calibration a statistic, and the map
held neither. Three minutes later he posted this paper
himself. It prices a question every judge of models
faces: one rule was run and its outcomes logged; what
would another rule have earned? Its answer refuses the
easy machine — split the data, judge on the part held
back — because when samples and dimensions grow together,
the held-back judge prices a smaller model than the one
that ships, and its rankings can turn over when the model
is fitted again on all the data. In its place the paper
corrects each prediction for the model's memory of its
own training points, and judges the full-data walk by its
own data: a stopping step chosen near-best on the walk,
and a doubly robust price of the new rule that stays true
even when the reward model's error does not fall away.
The cluster it opens (O) is wired on arrival — to the
course that builds gradient descent by hand (cluster M),
to the attention cluster whose budget follows the mass
(cluster J), to the lesson of the specification cluster on what
a judge can see (cluster I). And it hands the tracker a
mirror: the skim the quest itself made, sealed blind and scored on
arrival, is one of this paper's machines, rebuilt as a
habit. The weighing held on concepts, axis and questions;
the revision it foretold — the third foretold in a row —
never came.

Added by Paper 011 (2026-11-09, arXiv:2611.07088). The
fourth paper of the Thirst arrived minutes after the
third was sealed, while the third was still being taken
in; its seal carried the drift warning in writing, and
the warning came true word for word. The paper reads
minds from their mistakes: given a correct program and
one wrong answer, a language model proposes small changes
to the program until a changed program reproduces the
answer exactly, and the kept programs — the witnesses —
are read as symbolic models of the rule the answer came
from. With that instrument, people and large reasoning
models are read in one space on the Abstraction and
Reasoning Corpus, and the findings are the failure side
of the model's home territory: wrong answers crowd around
the right program; the wrong rules are simpler than the
right ones; a fifth of them fit only part of the evidence,
in the order the evidence stood; the deviations fall into
a few kinds, one carrying nearly half; people and
machines share every signature while failing on different
tasks — and the whole reading stands in fog, at a priced
rate, by the fact that many programs make one behavior.
For the first time in the Thirst, the foretold revision
landed: the count was right, the target wrong by one
scroll — 1.2.4, the capability profile, now carries the
pair of species in its jaggedness, the machines' shape
measurably not the human one. The model did not widen; it
grew deeper, in the territory it started from. The Oracle
rose on the full step of the band, from about 40 to about 50
percent: held ground was corrected by contact, which is
the fixed point's own signature, and the wiring ran
eleven links deep where five were foretold. What the
model still cannot do is named at Q51-Q53 — part the two
machines inside the satisficing reading, price the
witnesses against each other, and see the half of error
its instrument is built to miss.
