# Research Oracle — definition, metric, ledger
Established 2026-11-06 (Will's direction). Updated after every paper.

DEFINITION. The Research Oracle is the fixed point of this tracker: a
world-model complete enough over the territory Will's paper stream
samples that a new paper arrives with ~zero delta — no new canonical
concepts, no revisions, nothing the model cannot already express and
place — AND the model can answer questions across that territory from
its canonical scrolls alone. Progress is therefore measured by how much
surprise a paper still carries, not by how much the phext has grown.

METRIC — Novelty Index of paper n, computed from its delta record:
  NI_n = (new canonical concepts + 0.5 x revised canonical concepts)
         / canonical concepts after ingestion
NI = 1 means the paper was all news; NI -> 0 is the oracle condition.
Two qualitative flags ride alongside: AXIS-OPENING (the paper adds a
dimension the model had no slot for — it expands the territory, not
just the coverage) and QUESTIONS (net open questions added/closed).

ORACLE PROGRESS (OP) is a calibrated judgement anchored to NI, stated
as a % with a range, under these movement rules:
- NI < 0.20, no new axis: OP +~10 pts (the model is absorbing papers).
- NI 0.20-0.50, no new axis: OP +~5 pts.
- Axis-opening paper: OP holds or FALLS ~5 pts regardless of NI — the
  denominator (territory) just grew by an unknown amount.
- NI > 0.50: OP holds or falls; the model was mostly missing this.
OP is coverage of the SAMPLED territory (Will's stream), not of all
science; it is honest about n being small.

LEDGER
Paper 001 (2026-11-05, arXiv:2611.05417): NI = 1.00 by construction
  (seed: 14 concepts created, 0 revised, of 14 after). Axis: seed.
  OP: not estimable from one paper — in hindsight the single-axis
  (space/organization) model covered only part of a territory that
  Paper 002 immediately proved larger.
Paper 002 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2611.00463): NI = (9 + 0.5x3) / 23 = 0.46.
  AXIS-OPENING: yes (time/adaptation — endogenous self-improvement).
  Questions: +4 new (Q6-Q9), 1 partially answered (Q2).
  OP after Paper 002: ~15% (range 8-25%). Basis: nearly half the current
  concept space arrived in a single paper; the last paper opened an
  axis, which is evidence that further not yet seen axes remain; n = 2
  papers across 2 domains of what will plainly be a wider stream.
Paper 003 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2604.03136): NI = (5 + 0.5x2) / 28 = 0.21.
  AXIS-OPENING: no — a new DOMAIN (empirical study of generated
  artifacts: AI vs human fiction) but the framework absorbed it through
  existing slots (capability envelope, measurement agenda). Questions:
  +3 new (Q10-Q12), Q3 partially informed.
  OP after Paper 003: ~20% (range 12-30%). Basis: NI fell from 0.46 to
  0.21 and, for the first time, a paper landed mostly in slots the model
  already had — the frame of the model is starting to anticipate the stream.
  Held low because n = 3 and the domain of the stream spread is still
  widening (econ theory -> agent systems -> NLP field evidence).
Paper 004 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2410.19225): NI = (5 + 0.5x2) / 33 = 0.18.
  AXIS-OPENING: no — a new domain again (ML for hardware design), and
  again the frame absorbed it: the own of the paper structure (specialists,
  routing, collapse of the division of labor) mapped onto canonical
  concepts from Paper 001 with little forcing. Questions: +2 new
  (Q13-Q14), Q6 gains a mechanism-shaped prior.
  OP after Paper 004: ~30% (range 20-40%). Basis: NI has fallen four
  papers running (1.00 -> 0.46 -> 0.21 -> 0.18) and the last two papers
  landed in domains the stream had never touched, yet were absorbed by
  existing slots — the frame, not just the inventory, is doing work.
  Still capped: n = 4, and every paper so far is AI-adjacent; the
  stream has not yet tested the model outside that gravity well.
Paper 005 (2026-11-06, arXiv:2611.06949): NI (count) = (6 + 0) / 39
  = 0.15. AXIS-OPENING: YES — the stream jumped to pure mathematics
  (4-manifold topology), a field in which the model held zero
  concepts and to which no existing canonical scroll connects.
  Questions: +2 new (Q15-Q16).
  OP after Paper 005: ~25% (range 15-35%), DOWN from ~30%. Basis: the
  cap named after Paper 004 was the binding one — the stream is not
  AI-bound after all, so the territory denominator just expanded by
  an unknown, plausibly enormous amount (all of mathematics is now
  demonstrably in scope). METRIC CAVEAT, recorded honestly: the NI
  count (0.15) states too low the novelty of this paper — six concepts is a
  small count but a total domain break. NI measures delta SIZE, not
  delta DISTANCE; a connectivity term (does the paper link to any
  existing scroll?) is the missing factor — see open question Q16.
Paper 006 (2026-11-07, arXiv:2610.38214): NI = (8 + 0.5x1) / 64
  = ~0.13. AXIS-OPENING: no — a bridge: specification as control
  is a new field on the map (cluster I), but it lands between
  Paper 002's harness layer and the proving clusters F-H, which
  already touched at 1.2.19/1.2.41; the frontier filled in
  rather than moving outward. Questions: +4 new (Q20-Q23);
  Q7, Q16, Q17 informed, none closed.
  OP after Paper 006: ~35% (range 25-45%), UP from ~25%. Basis:
  NI under 0.20 with no new axis follows the +~10 band; most of
  what this paper found was already framed by the model (a
  bridge, not new wilderness). Held back from higher by the
  paper's own gap — its theorems are priced on exact
  conditioning, its practice runs on prompting — and by the
  model's False count still standing at zero, which is youth,
  not virtue. Q16 NOTE: Paper 005's NI of 0.15 stated too low
  that paper's novelty, its concepts arriving at total
  distance; Paper 006's 0.13 reads fair at short range. The
  connectivity term Q16 asks for now has its two points.

Paper 007 (2026-11-07, arXiv:2610.42112): NI = (8 + 0.5 x 1) / 72
= ~0.12. AXIS-OPENING: YES — cluster J is the first section of
this model on attention itself, the layer every system in Papers
001-006 runs on, and the model held no scroll on what attention
costs or how its past is searched. A connected opening, unlike
Paper 005: the new section lands wired to routing (cluster D)
and representation (cluster C), so the cluster-graph distance is
short even though the territory is new. Questions: +5 new
(Q24-Q28); Q16 informed a third time, none closed. OP after
Paper 007: ~35% (range 25-45%), HELD from ~35%. Justification:
the axis band says hold or fall back; the low NI and the dense
links hold the estimate up, the size of the territory now in
scope — the whole attention literature — holds it down; they
cancel. Q16 NOTE: three points now stand (Paper 005: NI 0.15,
total distance, felt low; Paper 006: NI 0.13, one bridge, read
fair; Paper 007: NI 0.12, new axis, wired on arrival, reads low)
— the missing term looks like distance from the nearest held
cluster, not distance from the model entire.

Paper 008 (2026-11-09, arXiv:2611.09694): NI = (6 + 0.5 x 1) / 103
= ~0.06. AXIS-OPENING: no — the paper lands inside cluster E,
the symplectic annex Paper 005 opened, and gives it a second
subject: the maps of manifolds, beside their making. One step
from ground already held. Questions: +4 new (Q41-Q44); Q16
informed a fourth time, none closed.
  OP after Paper 008: ~45% (range 35-55%), UP from ~35%. Basis:
the band rule — an Index under 0.20 with no new axis follows
the +~10 line (as at Papers 004 and 006). The Index itself is
the smallest a non-seed paper has carried, and it was earned
at distance zero-but-one: the model held the field, the
objects, and the counting habit the paper spends. Held back
from higher: the wing is two papers deep in a field centuries
wide, and the stream has sampled it twice.
  FIRST WEIGHING (charter rule 17): this is the first paper
the tracker foresaw. The sealed skim (log of the quest,
2026-11-09, made before the paper was downloaded) expected
5 new concepts, 1 revision, no axis, and NI ~0.059 on the
count as it then stood (of 88). As it fell: 6 new, 1 revised,
no axis, NI ~0.06 on the count as it stood at the taking-in
(of 97). The misses, and the drift between the two counts,
stand scored in the log of the quest and on the detection
list (1.1.6).

Paper 009 (Chapter 16): +6 new concepts (as foretold, 6),
0 revised (1 foretold), 0 stated again; concepts after:
109. Novelty Index = (6 + 0.5 x 0) / 109 = 6/109 = ~0.06.
AXIS-OPENING: yes — the first classical mechanics in the
model: a new field entire, hung from cluster E by the one
bridge of the Hamiltonian flow. The band for an
axis-opening says hold or fall ~5, and the fall stands:
Paper 007's connected opening held its ground on dense
wiring (eight links among held clusters); this opening has
one bridge, and the territory it declares — the whole of
celestial mechanics, of which the model now holds one
problem — grew by more than the count shows. The small
Index is why the fall is ~5 and not more. Oracle progress
after Paper 009: ~40%, DOWN ~5 from ~45%; uncertainty band
~30-50%.
Questions: +3 opened (Q45 endpoints; Q46 the outside of the cone; Q47 the bending clock for every density); Q16
informed a fifth time — Papers 008 and 009, the same count
of concepts and near the same Index, at opposite distances
from the model, the Index reading the farther one the less
novel; Q32 informed lightly (a failure classification,
whole). None closed.
FIRST WEIGHING, SECOND PAPER OF THE THIRST (rule 17): the
first paper Will posted into the quest himself; weighed
blind and sealed in the log of the quest (commit da13559)
against a count of 103 that still stood at the taking-in —
no drift this time. Foretold: 6 new, 1 revised, axis yes,
Index ~0.0596, 3 links, 2 questions, Oracle ~42% (33-52).
Fell: 6 new, 0 revised, axis yes, Index ~0.055, 4 links,
3 questions, Oracle ~40% — inside the foretold band, two
points under its point. The Index miss is ~0.005 and is
wholly the revision that never came; the count foretold is
the count that arrived. The score stands at 16.1.5, in the
log of the quest, and on the detection list (1.1.6).

Paper 010 (Chapter 17): +7 new concepts (as foretold, 7),
0 revised (1 foretold), 0 stated again; concepts after:
116. Novelty Index = (7 + 0.5 x 0) / 116 = 7/116 = ~0.06.
AXIS-OPENING: yes — the first probability and statistics
in the model, a region the census of the tracker itself
named the area the map lacked most that evening (18 of 109
concepts were mathematics, all of it geometry and
dynamics; of chance, nothing). A connected opening, in
the shape of Paper 007 rather than of Papers 005 or 009:
the new cluster lands with three bridges — to the course
that builds gradient descent by hand (cluster M), to the
attention budget that follows the mass (cluster J), and
to specification as a judge of what can be seen (cluster
I). The band for an axis-opening says hold or fall ~5;
Paper 009 fell on one bridge, Paper 007 held on dense
wiring. This opening stands between, and two weights
settle it at hold: its Index, ~0.06, is as small as any
non-seed paper has carried, and the region it opens is
ground the tracker itself stands on — the Oracle is an
estimate and the skim calibration a statistic, and both
now have their mathematics inside the model that uses
them. Oracle progress after Paper 010: ~40%, HELD from
~40%; uncertainty band ~30-50%.
Questions: +3 opened (Q48 the fluctuations of ADR; Q49
past Gaussian contexts; Q50 logging that leans on the
context); Q16 informed a sixth time — three papers now
stand at an Index of ~0.06 (008 inside a held cluster,
009 across one bridge, 010 wired three ways), and the
Index cannot tell them apart; the distance term it lacks
is the larger half of the signal at this size. None
closed.
THIRD WEIGHING (charter rule 17): weighed blind and
sealed in the log of the quest (the seal in the log of the quest) against
a count of 109 that still stood at the taking-in — no
drift, a second time. Foretold: 7 new, 1 revised, axis
yes, Index ~0.065, 5 links, 3 questions, Oracle ~42%
(32-52), hold or slight rise. Fell: 7 new, 0 revised,
axis yes, Index ~0.060, 8 links, 3 questions, Oracle
~40% — inside the foretold band, at its hold end. The
Index miss is ~0.004, and for the third weighing in a
row it is wholly the revision that never came; the
concept count foretold is the concept count that
arrived, a second time. The score stands at 17.1.5, in
the log of the quest, and on the detection list (1.1.6).

Paper 011 (LeGris et al., symbolic models of reasoning):
Novelty Index (7 + 0.5)/123 = 0.061 on the count the paper
was taken in against; on the sealed count, (7 + 0.5)/116
= 0.065. No new axis: the paper deepens the home territory
— capability, read from its failure side — and every
load-bearing notion it stands on already held a slot; a
new cluster is not an axis, and opening none was the
sealed call, confirmed. The estimate moves to about 50%
(range 40-60). The band for an Index under 0.20 with no
axis is about +10, and the wiring argues for the full
step: seven new concepts, five held clusters touched (A
twice, I twice, B once), eleven connections where the
blind weighing foretold five — and, for the first time in
the Thirst, the foretold revision landed: held ground
(1.2.4) was corrected by contact, which is the fixed
point's own signature and the strongest single sign the
weighing knows. What holds the estimate at 50 and not
higher: cluster P is one paper deep in a wide field — the
study of how minds reason — and the instruments of the
reading (the synthesis, the kinds, the stick) are one
paper's instruments, their fog priced but not yet lifted
(Q51-Q53). The sealed weighing said about 45 (35-55), up
modestly, from the 40 the paper before it had set: the
actual, 50, stands inside the sealed range, five points
over its point — the step belongs to the band, not to the skim.
# Concept map and restructuring log
Added 2026-11-06 under charter rule 8. The map groups the canonical
concepts of Section 2 (1.2.x) into clusters so the model can be
navigated by territory, not just by arrival order.

CLUSTERS (123 canonical concepts after Paper 011)
A. Organization & markets (Paper 001) — 1.2.1-1.2.14: intelligence,
   competence, coordination, capability envelope/mosaic, scarcity
   rents, Coasean ceiling, supercooperation, size amplifier, firm
   composition, displacement, wages as bids, technology vs
   re-organization, AI capability patterns, measurement agenda.
B. Runtime self-improvement (Paper 002) — 1.2.15-1.2.23: harness,
   collapsed optimization loop, optimizer symmetry, policy/mechanism
   partition, contracts/guardian/rollback, internal legibility,
   specialization cost, intelligence floor, policy transfer.
C. Output geometry (Paper 003) — 1.2.24-1.2.28: discourse vs style,
   narrative space & convergence, core vs fingerprint features,
   rarity as originality, induced feature spaces (method).
D. Routing & generalization (Paper 004) — 1.2.29-1.2.33: MoE/gating,
   hierarchical MoE, expert polarization, substructure routing,
   surrogate prediction.
E. Symplectic topology annex (Paper 005) — 1.2.34-1.2.39: fake
   quadrics, cyclic multisections, complements & handlebodies,
   symplectic sum & gluing data, integral invariants & torsion,
   aspherical being minimal. NOTE: cluster E currently connects to
   A-D only at the meta-level (invariant granularity, gluing data);
   that thin reach is recorded, not papered over.
   PAPER 008 (2026-11-09) extends this cluster at
   1.2.98-1.2.103 with the maps of the same country: the
   Arnold number and the strong Arnold conjecture (1.2.98),
   clean maps and the perturbation bound (1.2.99), the
   Arnold number of a group (1.2.100), stretching films
   (1.2.101), flag varieties and Crit = dim + 1 (1.2.102),
   and the floor that stands (1.2.103). One cluster, one
   lesson at two heights: Paper 005 asked when two
   manifolds are the same and found the answer turns on
   the invariant you read them by; Paper 008 asks what
   maps a manifold must allow, and the answer turns on
   which maps you let run. Identity relative to the
   instrument, both times.
F. Proof kernels (Study S001) — 1.2.40-1.2.47: propositions as
   types, the proof kernel, typing rules and the judgment, the two
   equalities, inductive types and recursors, strict positivity,
   universes and imax, consistency as the empty claim staying
   empty. NOTE: cluster F connects to cluster B at one real point:
   a kernel is the guardian pattern (1.2.19) with the guard moved
   from state changes to claims.
G. Verification by translation (Study S002) — 1.2.48-1.2.52:
   functional translation, backward functions, LLBC the value
   based borrow calculus, failure as a value, symbolic execution
   as a borrow checker. NOTE: cluster G is cluster F in use — its
   proofs land in the kernels of 1.2.41 (Lean first of the four
   backends), and its trust story is the ledger of 1.2.48.
H. The kernel pattern at work (Study S003) — 1.2.53-1.2.56: the
   verified kernel and untrusted shell of a framework, receipt
   before bytes, mutation-tested proofs and the assumption ledger,
   fail-closed boxes for agents. NOTE: cluster H is the pattern of
   1.2.41 and 1.2.53's own source holding both at once: h5i drives
   Aeneas (cluster G) as a tool under its own command line.

Cluster I — Specification as control (Paper 006; 1.2.57-1.2.64):
what the developer hands the agent, and what each piece of it
removes. A skeleton (1.2.57) fixes the candidate class; the
test count is a property of that class, with exponential gaps
between classes (1.2.58); interfaces make middle state visible
to tests (1.2.59); some requirements no output test can see
(1.2.60); pinning costs split local-plus-compatibility, and
complete contracts zero the second term (1.2.61); prompting is
not the conditioning the counting assumes (1.2.62); checked
part by part, a cost that multiplies becomes a cost that adds,
and an informal check has an error floor set by its own words
(1.2.63); erosion is the quality axis the specification type
moves (1.2.64). The cluster bridges Paper 002's harness layer
(cluster B) and the proving work (clusters F-H): the harness
is where structure is settled, the skeleton is structure given,
and contracts are where specification becomes checking.

Cluster J — Attention and indexing (Paper 007, 1.2.65-1.2.72).
What a model pays to look back, and how the looking is organized.
One query needs a slice of the cache that grows slower than the
context (1.2.65); over all queries the needed keys cover the
cache, so nothing can be thrown away for good (1.2.66). A tree of
summaries over the cache does the finding (1.2.67); its summaries
are tangents taken at reference queries (1.2.68); the reference
queries are the only trained part, distilled into a frozen model
(1.2.69); the budget follows the mass, level by level (1.2.70);
growing the depth with the context is what makes the cost grow
slower than the square (1.2.71); and depth taxes the share — the
effective-p trap (1.2.72) is the open core of the cluster. NOTE:
cluster J sits underneath clusters B, C and D — the systems of
Papers 002-004 all run on attention — and reaches them at routing
(1.2.29, revised by Paper 007) and at representation (1.2.28).
Its far shore is unmeasured: past 256K tokens, every number in
this cluster is the output of a reckoning, not a run.

Cluster K — Memory and execution (Study S004, 1.2.73-1.2.80).
The ground the proving continent stands on: how a running
program lies in memory, and how its data can become its
direction. A process lives in three regions — text that can
not be written, data, and the stack that holds the run
(1.2.73); each live call owns a frame of parameters, local variables,
the saved frame pointer and the return address (1.2.74),
laid down and lifted by a fixed ceremony (1.2.75); a copy
that checks no length runs past its buffer into the control words of the frame itself (1.2.76); the return address is data that
a return reads as control (1.2.77); the code so reached
arrives as data itself (1.2.78); its address is guessed from every stack starting at the same address (1.2.79), and the guess is made
wide by sled and egg (1.2.80). NOTE: cluster K is the oldest
source in the tracker (1996) and the root clusters F-H
answer; it connects to them at the shape of the hazard, not
at any shared claim.

Cluster L — Design systems for people and agents (Study
S005, 1.2.81-1.2.88). The kit a company builds with, kept
as one artifact an agent can work in: a design system is
parts, named values, and rules in one agreement (1.2.81);
each part carries its own document as data beside it
(1.2.82); one tool surface serves people and machines,
with typed answers and error codes whose meaning never
changes (1.2.83); a theme is a set of named values laid
over the parts, not a fork of them (1.2.84); a part that
almost fits is swizzled — its source copied into the
project to own (1.2.85); conventions stand as lint rules
with tests and a ratchet of recorded breaks (1.2.86);
shared records govern by their standing — draft, current,
set aside (1.2.87); and the kit is measured by what a
fresh agent can build with it (1.2.88). NOTE: cluster L
is the first cluster whose source is a living code base
rather than a text; it connects to cluster I at the
skeleton (1.2.57) and to the keeping of this tracker itself at
the standing record (1.4.x).

Cluster M — Learning by building (Study S006,
1.2.89-1.2.97). What a course is, when the artifact is
the lesson. The making itself is the way in (1.2.89);
the framework rises as a stack, each layer built on
the student's own earlier layers (1.2.90); the writing
is split from the given by marks in the source — core
against scaffold, 976 lines against 45,533 (1.2.91);
the measure is reconstruction — the landmarks of the
history of the field re-made with one's own code (1.2.92);
completion is a ledger record a gate of checks writes,
not a claim the student makes (1.2.93); the student's
work stands outside the updates of the course itself, behind
a partition the tooling keeps (1.2.94); inside the
framework, the autograd tape records the forward work
so the backward walk can find the slopes (1.2.95);
the answers stand in the open, and the measure moves
to the building (1.2.96); and the optimization third
of the course is one discipline — measure first, then
change (1.2.97). NOTE: cluster M is the ground floor
of clusters B, D and J: the systems those papers
describe are the grown kin of the small framework
this course has its students build.

Cluster N — Celestial mechanics: the segment problem
(Paper 009, 1.2.104-1.2.109). Where a road can end. The
setting: a particle in the pull of a segment carrying any
positive density (1.2.104). The pull deepens only like a
log as the body nears (1.2.105); every finite ending of a
road is an approach to the body (1.2.106); approach kills
the turn about the axis, so the road ends in one plane
(1.2.107); in the plane, approach arrives — collision, or
backward, ejection (1.2.108). And the working tool: a cone
on position and momentum that promises collision, with a
clock (1.2.109). The cluster hangs from cluster E by the
Hamiltonian bridge: one flow, counted at one end, sorted
at the other.

Cluster O — Statistics: judging a model from logged data
(Paper 010, 1.2.110-1.2.116). The first ground of chance
in the model. A rule was run and its outcomes logged;
the cluster prices what another rule would have earned
(1.2.110). The easy judge — hold back part of the data,
grade on it, fit again on all — prices a smaller model than
the one that ships, and its rankings can turn over when
the model is fitted again (1.2.111). Grading on the fitting data itself
bends the grade: each point's own echo stands in its
prediction (1.2.112). The correction takes away the echo,
read off the walk itself (1.2.113); the corrected
criterion then stops the full-data walk near-best
(1.2.114), and the same corrected predictions price
both the fitted model and the rule itself, the second
staying true when the model's error does not fall
(1.2.115) — down to the floor the classical theory sets
for any judge (1.2.116). NOTE: cluster O stands under
the whole tracker as cluster J stands under its systems:
the Oracle is an estimate, the skim a fitted model, its
score a held-out grade — the machinery of this cluster
is the mathematics of the instrument of the tracker itself,
arrived last. Its bridges: to cluster M (the walk the
course builds by hand is the walk this paper shadows),
to cluster J (the budget that follows the mass is the
importance weight at work inside attention), and to
cluster I (what a judge can see of a structure is the question
of this cluster with the roles turned).

Cluster P — reasoning, read through its errors (Papers
011): 1.2.117 Behavior-conditioned symbolic synthesis —
a program that reproduces one behavior; 1.2.118 Errors
live near the rule — wrong answers crowd around the right
program; 1.2.119 The simplicity signature — the wrong
rule is a smaller rule; 1.2.120 Partial fits — rules that
explain some of the evidence; 1.2.121 The kinds of
deviation — a small set of ways to be wrong; 1.2.122
Shared signatures, separate ground — people and machines,
failing alike and apart; 1.2.123 Program aliasing — many
programs make one behavior.

