# Research Oracle — detection list
Stood up 2026-11-09 under charter rule 17. The ledger (1.1.4) holds
the estimate; this scroll holds the SIGNS watched for the fixed
point itself — the shapes that say the model is closing on the
territory of the stream, apart from any one paper's estimate.

SIGN ONE — THE RUN OF THE NOVELTY INDEX. Paper by paper: 1.00
(001, the seed), 0.46 (002), 0.21 (003), 0.18 (004), 0.15 (005),
0.13 (006), 0.12 (007), 0.06 (008), 0.06 (009), 0.06 (010),
0.06 (011). The run falls, and the fall is the first
shape of the fixed point: each paper arrives carrying less the
model cannot already say. The watch: an Index that jumps back up
is no failure — distance, not size, is what the Index misses
(Q16), so a jump reads as a far paper landing, and the reading
is checked against where it lands.

SIGN TWO — AXIS EVENTS. Papers that opened an axis: 002 (time —
a system rewriting the machinery that runs it), 005 (a new field
entire — pure mathematics), 007 (attention — the layer under
every system the model holds), 009 (mechanics —
where a falling road can end). Four of nine papers — Paper
008, landing inside a held cluster, opened none. The watch:
the fixed point nears as the stream stops finding layers and
fields the model has no slot for. Axis events should grow rare,
and the ones that still come should land wired to clusters
already held, as 007 did — not at total distance, as 005 did.
Paper 009 (2026-11-09) opened the fourth axis: classical
mechanics, entire, from one problem — not at total
distance (the Hamiltonian ground was already held), and
not densely wired: one bridge, against 007's eight links.
Between the two shapes the watch has so far seen.
Paper 010 (2026-11-09) opened the fifth axis:
probability and statistics — a field the model held
nothing of, named the area the map lacked most by the census
of the tracker the same evening it arrived. The
landing is the wired shape, further along it than 007:
three bridges stand (to clusters M, J and I), and the
field is the ground the model itself runs on — its own
estimate and its own calibration are citizens of the
new territory. The reading of the watch: axes still come,
but they now come as ground the model was already
standing on without holding — the census saw this one
before it landed, which no earlier axis allowed.

Paper 011 opened no axis either, and its shape is the
other pole of the watch: a paper that opens nothing, makes
nothing wider — and still corrects held ground (1.2.4). Between
Paper 008 (inside a held cluster, revising it) and Paper
011 (a new cluster on the home territory, revising the
home cluster), the watch now holds both shapes of a map made deeper.
The axis count stands at five; the openings remain the
events the model cannot schedule.

SIGN THREE — SKIM CALIBRATION (charter rule 17). From
2026-11-09, every paper the daily quest picks is weighed blind —
title and abstract alone, before the paper is downloaded: the
new concepts, revisions, and axis call it is expected to bring,
the Novelty Index those numbers give, the links and questions
it is expected to bring, and the Oracle estimate expected to
stand after it. The AFTER record weighs the same fields as they
fell. The calibration is two numbers: the mean size of the miss
in the expected Index over the last ten papers weighed, and the
share of axis calls called right.
