# Areas — the map of this phext (charter rule 16)
Built 2026-11-08 at Will's direction (Restructure Pass #4).
This phext is kept in AREAS: five standing indexes, each
gathering one kind of thing from wherever in the phext it
lives, so a reader can walk the whole by kind and not only
by chapter. The areas hold pointers and one-line accounts;
the claims themselves stay where they were made — in the
concept scrolls (1.2.x), the chapters (2-12), the
foundations (1.3.x), and the standing record (1.4.x).
Nothing was moved to build the areas, and nothing was
pruned: an area is a way of seeing the phext, laid over it.

THE FIVE AREAS
- STATE MACHINES (1.5.2): every machine the rule-13 passes
  have drawn out of the works taken in, and the machines
  found in work taken in before rule 13 stood.
- STATEMENTS (1.5.3): what a statement is in this phext,
  the four ways of standing, the grounds that give them, and the
  steps by which a standing may change — the record itself
  stands at 1.4.x.
- REFERENCE (1.5.4): the foundations and defined words of
  Section 3, and the standoff companions of the chapters,
  presented as one reference area.
- THE PATH (1.5.5): every paper and study in the order it
  was submitted, with the Oracle numbers after each paper
  and the restructuring passes where they fell.
- RESEARCHERS (1.5.6): the people behind the works taken
  in — their works here, and the standing of the author
  check of rule 15 for each.

THE MAKING, PLAINLY: how this Oracle was built — intake,
delta, standing, proof, reshaping — is set out at 1.5.7
and 1.5.8, in level-zero words, as this phext's own map of
itself.

UPKEEP (rule 16): every taking-in and every restructuring
pass updates the areas it touches in the same pass — new
machines join the machines area, new names join the
researchers, the path gains its entry, the counts of the
statements area are made fresh. An area that has fallen
behind the phext is a defect, audited at each pass.
# Area: State machines
Every machine named in this phext, gathered from the
chapters that hold it. A state machine is defined at
1.3.85: a set of states, the moves between them, and the
rules — stated or not — about which moves are allowed.
Charter rule 13 (from 2026-11-07) makes every taking-in
draw out the machines its work holds. The machines stand
in three kinds: STATED — the work itself states the
machine; INFERRED — Emise reads the machine out of the
work, marked [synthesis] where it stands in its chapter;
FOUND — the work of Chapters 2-9, taken in before rule 13
stood, holds the machine in plain sight, but no pass ever
drew it out; these are noted as found, not drawn out, and
no gap test has been run on them. The gap named for a
machine is the difference between the machine as intended
and the machine as it is actually built or run.

DRAWN OUT UNDER RULE 13 — STATED BY THE WORK
Paper 006 (Chapter 10; the review stands at 10.1.2):
M1 — the two-step generation machine. States: no
structure committed, the agent spread over every program
it can make; then inside one class of programs. Moves: a
structure is committed — by the agent's own choice, or by
a skeleton the developer gives — and tests then select
inside the class. Gap, stated by the paper: a developer
who gives only tests does not make the first move and
cannot see it, and the tests bend that move toward the
structure that says least; under the wrong skeleton,
2^d - d programs are left standing. Reusable form:
concept 1.2.58.
M2 — the chain machine of Theorem 2.2. States: one
carried bit, handed stage to stage down a chain of n
stages; each stage is a function of a fresh input and the
carried bit, and the last state is the output. Gap: the
middle states are hidden from the end — a fault must
damage the carried state and live to the output, or an
end test notes nothing. Home: 10.1.2, and the worked walk
at 10.1.3; concept 1.2.59.
M3 — the specification loop of the paper's third part.
States: a candidate drawn; checked; taken, or drawn
again. Open, the loop runs once — a statement of
structure as a starting condition, one draw. Closed, a
checker guards the taking move. Gap: the guard's
false-take rate prices the loop, and the part of that
rate set by the words of the contract does not fall when
the judge improves. Home: 10.1.2; concept 1.2.63.
M4 — the repair loop: the loop of M3 as the paper
measures it, in rounds of failing tests handed to a fresh
conversation, the tests standing as the checker. Its
drift over rounds is erosion. Home: 10.1.2;
concept 1.2.64.

Paper 007 (Chapter 11; the review stands at 11.1.2):
M1 — the router. State: a level of the tree, and the set
of nodes still open. Move: score the summaries of the
open nodes, keep the smallest set whose weighed mass
reaches the share p, open the children of the kept set;
at the leaves, attend over the keys that lived. The same
machine runs at every level with the same p. Gap: that
running one p at every level is what sets the trap
of concept 1.2.72. Home: 11.1.2;
concept 1.2.70.
M2 — the tree keeps itself. State: a stack of open
buffers, one per level. Move: a token arrives; when C new
keys stand in a row their first-level summaries close,
and a summary of each higher level closes every
C-to-that-level tokens — a doubling clock, most steps
closing nothing. Gap: none named by the paper; the upkeep
is put near one part in a hundred by a reckoning (1.3.99),
not by a run. Home: 11.1.2; concept 1.2.67.
M3 — the making of a converted model. States: the model
frozen; the tree taught — at each level the tree's
weighed shares are matched to the model's own attention,
only the reference queries trained, all children selected
while the match is poor and the selection cut to the
top-p rule as the match improves; the converted model.
Gap, from the paper's own aside: models under a billion
parameters converted poorly — the taught part can only
copy attention that is worth copying. Home: 11.1.2;
concept 1.2.69.

Study S004 (Chapter 12; the review stands at 12.2.1):
M1 — the call machine, stated by the article outright.
States: the chain of live frames on the stack, one for
each call not yet returned. Moves: CALL — push the
parameters, push the return address, run the opening
ceremony: the chain grows by one frame. RETURN — run the
closing code, take the return address as the next
instruction: the chain shrinks by one frame, and running
resumes in the caller. No other move is in the machine,
and no write to a local variable is a move at all. Home:
12.2.1; concepts 1.2.74 and 1.2.75.
M3 — the building of an exploit, the progression the
article itself states as its method; calling it a
machine is the lens of this tracker, and the chapter
says so. Five
stages: learn the layout of the frame; set code down as
data inside a buffer; aim the return address back into
the buffer; guess the address from one's own stack
pointer and search the offset; make the aim wide — a
sled to land on, an egg to ride in. Each stage takes away
one unknown the stage before it left. Home: 12.2.1;
concepts 1.2.78-1.2.80.
Study S005 (Chapter 13; the review stands at 13.2.1):
M1 — the making state of a text field, stated by the
work in its design record of the IME guard. States: a
character is being built through an input method, or it
is not. Moves: the building starts, the building ends;
while the building stands, the keys that would give
orders are part of the character, not orders. Two
signals tell the state: the building flag, and the old
code 229. The record names its gap: seven guarded
places in the core, three by a copied test, and one lab
editor guarding a different path, its Escape and Tab
with no guard. Home: 13.2.1.
M2 — the standing of a record, stated in AGENTS.md.
States: draft, current, set aside. Moves: approval
makes a record current; a newer record sets an old one
aside. The moves that do NOT count are named: code
merged, quiet in review, an old wiki page. Only a
current record governs. Home: 13.2.1; concept 1.2.87.
M3 — the stack of layers, stated in the family record
for the closing of layers. State: the pile of open
layers over a page. Move: a closing key is taken by the
top layer only; a dialog gives up its close to a layer
above it. Home: 13.2.1.

Study S006 (Chapter 14; the review stands at 14.2.1):
M1 — the path of a module. States: locked, ready,
started, completed. The state that counts is read off
the ledger (.tito/progress.json); the completion of the next
module is refused until the one before it stands
completed in the ledger. Home: 14.2.1.
M2 — the gate inside the complete command. In order:
the tests inside the notebook; a check of the shape of the notebook;
the export into the student's package; the tests of
the package as imported; the writing of the ledger;
the checking of the milestones. A failure stops the
line where it stands. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.93.
M3 — the unlocking of a milestone. Each landmark is
cut into parts; each part names the modules it needs;
the landmark stands complete only when every part
stands passed on the student's code. Run with checks
skipped, it is a demo and records nothing. Home:
14.2.1; concept 1.2.92.
M4 — the release tiers. The policy reads the role of a region (core, scaffold, challenge, instructor) and the
tier being made (student, instructor), and gives one
move: clear, keep, or take out. Two roles stand in
the policy with no region carrying them in the
sources. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.91.
M5 — the update partition. Two lists in the code:
what an update may write over, and what must stand
(the notebooks, the environment, the .tito folder,
the built core). A snapshot of the kept heart is
taken before the update; an unreachable release
changes nothing. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.94.
M6 — the autograd tape. With tracking off, an
operation computes and is forgotten; with it on and
a gradient asked, the operation is recorded as a
node; the backward walk visits the nodes in the
opposite order of their making and lets the tape go
in one pass at the end. Home: 14.2.1; concept 1.2.95.
Paper 008 (Chapter 15; the review stands at 15.1.2):
M1 — the perturbation machine. States: a clean
Hamiltonian diffeomorphism, whose fixed points form a
Morse-Bott submanifold N; then a small turn of the map.
Moves: the turn trades N for exactly Crit(N) fixed
points. The machine is Proposition 2.2 of the paper;
The model of Pozniak of a neighborhood of the diagonal is
the tool its proof stands on. Its named gap: the
fixed points it gives may be degenerate — gathered on
the locus, not spread through M. That is no defect of
the machine; it is the product. The strong Arnold
conjecture (1.2.98) is exactly the claim that the
machine cannot be run under Crit(M), and it is the
claim that fell. Home: 15.1.2; concept 1.2.99.
M2 — the film-stretching search. States: the elements
of a clean group action, named by their blocks of
directions that share an eigenvalue — the blocks the
map turns, holds, and flips.
Moves: stretch one film — turn one turning block all
the way to a hold or a flip. Guard: Crit(Fix) never
rises under the move (the paper's counts: sets of
three and pairs, each at least zero). End: every block a hold or
a flip, the split balanced — the least Crit(Fix) the
group gives. Home: 15.1.2; concept 1.2.101.

Paper 009 (Chapter 16; the review stands at 16.1.2):
M1 — the flow and its endings. States: the states outside
the segment, each with its kept energy and its kept turn
ℓ. Moves: the Hamiltonian flow. The finite ends of the run pass
through one gate only — approach (δ → 0) — and the second door of the gate, the ℓ-gate, passes only ℓ = 0 into the plane,
whose roads land. The forbidden state — an end with the
particle standing clear — is proved unreachable. Guard:
negative energy closes escape to infinity as well. Home:
16.1.2; concepts 1.2.106-1.2.108.
M2 — the cone trap. States: in the cone C(q*) or out of
it. Moves: the flow in the plane; once in, never out — the
sight-point of the momentum is drawn into the reach of the segment
and the position holds in the triangle. Guard: inward
momentum (p_ρ < 0, or the time-shift). Exit: the floor
only — collision, by the clock −ρ0/p_ρ0. Gap: the outside
has no map; the trap certifies what it holds and is
silent on the rest. Home: 16.1.2; concept 1.2.109.

Paper 010 (Chapter 17; the review stands at 17.1.2):
M1 — the split judge. States: the logged record whole;
the record split; a model fitted on one part; the model
judged on the part it did not see; the model fitted again on
the whole record and shipped. Gap, stated by the paper:
the last move breaks the judgment — the judge priced a
model fitted on less, and its ranking need not survive
the fitting again, because the risk itself moves with the share
of the record fitted on (concept 1.2.111). Home: 17.1.2.
M2 — the same-record machine with its correction (the
paper's own pipeline). States: the logged record; one
gradient-descent walk per arm over the whole record; the
states of the walk corrected — the own echo of each
training round taken away; the criterion read off the corrected states;
a stopping step chosen per arm; the value priced twice
from the same corrected predictions — the fitted model's
(ADM) and the own price of the rule (ADR). Guards: the logging rule
known, every action kept possible, contexts from the
bell law, rewards of one index each. Home: 17.1.2;
concepts 1.2.113-1.2.115.
M3 — state evolution, the Gaussian shadow. States: a
growing table of how the states of the walk at each
pair of steps lean on each other, on the direction of
the truth, and on the index of the target rule. Moves:
the recursion — each next state carried as a Gaussian
draw leaning as the table says, the table updated from
the draw. Gap: the shadow is proved for contexts from the
bell law; outside them it is run, not proved (Q49).
Home: 17.1.2; concept 1.2.113.

PAPER 011 (LeGris, Mondorf, Jin, Jagadish, Lake and
Gureckis, Chapter 18 — reasoning, read through its
errors):

M1 — the synthesis loop (the paper's central machine).
States: a reference program and one observed wrong answer
in hand; a proposed set of line changes; a changed
program; the program cut down to the lines its output
hangs on; a run; kept or set aside. Transitions: the
proposing model (a large language model) proposes changes,
conditioned on the one answer; the set is applied within
its budget; the dead lines are cut; the program runs on
the test input; it is kept if and only if its output
matches the answer within the noise allowance (two cells,
on grids of ten or more). Guards: changes drawn from the
small language only (1.3.142); programs in the normal
form; fifteen tries per answer across three budgets.
Intended: recover the rule the answer came from. Actual:
recover a program that makes the answer. The gap is
priced by the paper's own validation: the keep test
certifies a witness, never the rule (1.2.123) — an
intended-actual gap of the space's shape, not of the
the making of the loop. Home: 18.1.2; concept 1.2.117.

M2 — the search of the solver, as the paper reads it.
States: no rule in hand; a candidate rule; the examples
checked so far, in the order they stand; a rule settled
for; a rule given up, another formed. Transitions: form a
candidate from the examples met so far; check against the
next example in order; on a miss, form another; on a
good-enough run of fits, settle and answer. Guards: the
good-enough mark is a threshold, not a full check (1.3.144).
Intended: find the rule. Actual: find a rule that passes
the mark — the partial fits of 1.2.120 and the shorter
rules of 1.2.119 are its shadow. The gap stands open by
the paper's own statement: formed narrow and verified
narrow leave one shadow (Q51). Home: 18.1.2; concepts
1.2.119, 1.2.120.

M3 — the effort dial. States: a budget of reasoning set
before the task is met; the search run under it; the
answer. Transitions: the budget is set (low, medium,
high, in the machine settings); the search runs; every
signature of cluster P moves with the setting — capture,
shortening, the share of partial fits, the dominant kind,
the distance of the machine's ground from people's
(1.2.122). Guards: within one model, the dial is the only
thing changed. Intended: measure what more reasoning
buys. Actual: across models and people, the dial and the
capability of the solver stand mixed in the readings —
the paper's own stated gap. Home: 18.1.2; concept 1.2.122.

DRAWN OUT UNDER RULE 13 — INFERRED, MARKED [synthesis]
M5 (Paper 006) — the drift machine of a long-lived code
base. States: code bases mixing what was meant with what
happened by accident. Each patch move keeps the accidents
— the tests guard them — and piles new structure on top;
a standing skeleton changes the moves, letting a feature
be made again against stated choices. Erosion (1.2.64) is the
drift measure. Home: 10.2.1. [synthesis]
M6 (Paper 006) — the developer's blind machine. The agent
runs the two-step machine of M1, but its structure state
is hidden from the developer, who sees only the test
outcomes at the end: the chain machine of M2 with the
developer on the wrong side of the interface. Home:
10.2.1. [synthesis]
M4 (Paper 007) — the governor the paper needs and does
not build. Its failure states are the two attractors of
concept 1.2.72: the share held fixed while the context
grows, so the kept set stops growing; and the share
pushed toward one, so the top levels keep all. Its moves
are named: grow a level as the context grows, and let the
kept share rise with depth. The paper hands the governor
its input free — the per-level powers can be weighed
while the model runs. Home: 11.2.1. [synthesis]
M5 (Paper 007) — two ways to keep an index. A built
index: groups fixed by position, summaries made again
from the keys as they arrive — it cannot drift, and
cannot follow meaning across positions. A learned index:
keys grouped in a learned space, the groups moving with
training — it can follow meaning, and can lose its shape
when the text shifts kind. No run known to this tracker
sets the two on the same model and the same tests (Q26).
Home: 11.2.1. [synthesis]
M2 (Study S004) — the encoded machine: the rule-13 lens
on M1 of the same chapter. The words of a frame lie side
by side in one region that can be written: the local
variables, the saved frame pointer, the return address.
A copy into a local buffer, kept going past the end of
the buffer, writes over the control words of M1 itself — a move
M1 does not hold: from any state, a long enough write
through a local buffer rewrites where the next RETURN
goes. Data rewriting control (1.2.77). Home: 12.2.1.
[synthesis]
M4 (Study S005) — the light and dark reading. One set of
named values, two readings: the state is which reading
the page stands under, and the move is a change of the
reading at the top — never inside a part, for the parts
hold no state of it. The work states the values and the
layers; the machine is the lens of this tracker. Home:
13.2.1. [synthesis]

M7 (Study S006) — the two courses in one source. One
module file, two starts: the default hands out the
solved notebook; the exercise start clears the core.
The ledger does not record which mode a completion
came by. The work ships both and states each; the
machine that joins them is the lens of this tracker.
Home: 14.2.1. [synthesis]
M3 (Paper 008) — the group-search bound, read as one
machine. States: a group of candidate maps; for each,
a price — Crit of its fixed locus; at the end, the
least price, written down as a bound on Arn(M). The
machine prices Arn of the group (1.2.100), not Arn of
the manifold: its bound is exact only where a floor
from outside (1.2.103) meets it, as on the quadrics,
and a wide field stands open everywhere else (Q41).
The work states the search and its pieces; the
machine, as one machine, is the lens of this tracker.
Home: 15.2.1. [synthesis]

Paper 009 (Chapter 16; the review stands at 16.2.1):
M3 — the screening machine. States: the state of a craft near a
long body, read against the body's segment. Moves: three
checks in order — the turn (ℓ not zero bars collision),
the cone (inside with inward momentum: collision certain,
with its clock), and silence (outside: no verdict). Gap:
the screen is one-sided — silence is not safety, and
reading it as safety adds a theorem the paper does not
hold. Home: 16.2.1. [synthesis]
M4 (Paper 010) — the instrument of the tracker itself, read as
one of Paper 010's machines. States: a skim fitted on
the papers already taken in; a new paper arriving by the
stream; the skim scored against the paper it never saw.
Moves: weigh blind and seal; read; score whole. The
discipline of the quest is the split judge (Paper 010, M1)
rebuilt as a habit: the BEFORE record is a fitted model,
the AFTER record its held-out grade — and Paper 010
prices what that habit cannot buy: the judge prices the
skim at the size it had when it weighed, while the model
the score describes has already grown by the paper being
scored. Whether the regime of the tracker itself is the
proportional one — papers against concepts, as samples
against dimensions — is the machine's open edge. Home:
17.2.1. [synthesis]

FOUND IN WORK TAKEN IN BEFORE RULE 13 — NOTED, NOT DRAWN OUT
Paper 001 (Chapter 2) — the patterns of composition and
the threshold. States: the patterns the making of a firm
can take — mixed or separate, human or AI-only (1.2.9). Move:
cross the supercooperation threshold (1.2.7) and the sign
of returns to scale flips — the paper's own name for the
move is a phase transition — and displacement then runs
sudden across firms in place of step by step inside them
(1.2.10). The paper names the states and the threshold
itself; no rule-13 pass has drawn the machine out or
tested it for gaps.
Paper 002 (Chapter 3) — the replacement machine. State:
s = (durable data, sandbox, active policy), stated by the
paper in so many words. Moves: an edit proposes a
candidate policy; the guardian — standing outside the
policy it judges — answers 0 or 1; only a 1 makes the
candidate active. Running back is independent for each of
the three parts of the state, and the history is
append-only: what has been written
stays written. Home: concept 1.2.19; the assembly at 3.1.2.
Paper 002 (Chapter 3) — the loop that closes on itself.
The worker runs under its harness; the cost-annotated
history grows; the worker rewrites the policy of the
harness; the guardian of the replacement machine gates
the swap. Turned end to end, that loop is the collapsed
optimization loop: improvement made inside the run in
place of between runs. Home: concepts 1.2.15 and 1.2.16.
Paper 004 (Chapter 5) — the routing machine. For each
input, gates at three grains of the program graph weigh
their experts, a higher gate weighs the three grains, and
the output is the weighed sums. The machine's slow state
is the shape of the experts themselves: like inputs
routed alike, so the experts grow unlike — a shape made
by the routing and the training together, not assigned.
Home: concepts 1.2.29 and 1.2.30.
Paper 004 (Chapter 5) — polarization and the two-stage
guard. States over training: the pool of experts in
balanced use, or collapsed onto one. The move between
them compounds: an early lead earns more weight, more
weight earns more training. The guards the paper builds:
a balance pressure on the spread of the weights, and
two-stage training — each expert made able alone before
any gating stands, and kept rehearsing alone while it
stands.
Home: concept 1.2.31.
Study S001 (Chapter 7) — the two small machines of
MiniLean4. The checker: a term is judged by computation
steps, run under a stated fuel — 20,000 steps, as the
book's chapter gives it and the study checked (7.1.2).
The tactic layer: the state is the set of goals standing;
a tactic is a move that rewrites that state, until no
goals stand. Homes: concepts 1.2.41 and 1.2.42;
foundations 1.3.75 and 1.3.78.
Study S002 (Chapter 8) — the symbolic interpreter.
State: a symbolic state of loans, values standing as
symbols. Moves: carry out the statements of the borrow
calculus on the stand-ins; where calls, the arms of a
choice, or the heads of loops meet, states are gathered
into bundles and merged by the join. Every run ends in a
value — ok, fail, or div. Homes: concepts 1.2.52, 1.2.49
and 1.2.51.
Study S003 (Chapter 9) — receipt before bytes. A gate of
two moves on every request through the proxy: the receipt
is written first — what was asked, under which policy,
allowed or not — and only then do the bytes move. If the
receipt cannot be written, the request does not go. Home:
concept 1.2.54.
Study S003 (Chapter 9) — the fail-closed box. Two
states: set up exactly as stated, or not run at all. The
machine has no third state — no running weaker than
stated. Home: concept 1.2.56.

M4 (Paper 011) — the machine-made measuring stick.
States: a task with no reference program; a search for any
program fitting the shown pairs; the shortest found,
standing as the stick; every kept error program read as a
distance from the stick. Transitions: search; keep the
shortest correct program; measure all deviations against
it. Guards: the stick must fit every shown pair.
Intended: a neutral ruler. Actual: the readings of the
evaluation split are distances from a found program, not
from the rule — the hand-written stick of the training
split and the machine-made stick of the evaluation split
are two sticks with one ruler printed on them, and the
splits differ where a stick could move the reading (the
shaped share of partial fits, 62.5 against 48.5 percent).
The paper states the fact of the two sticks and runs its
effects on both; the machine is the reading this tracker makes of
what the fact does. Home: 18.2.1. [synthesis]

SWEPT, NO MACHINE FOUND
Chapter 4 (Paper 003): its way of working is one pass —
one prompt held fixed, stories written, features drawn
out of the differences, the geometry read off. Nothing in
it keeps a state that later steps consult, so no machine
in the sense of 1.3.85 was found.
Chapter 6 (Paper 005): the paper builds by recipes —
pieces cut, complements taken, sums glued by named maps.
The steps are ordered, but no state is carried that
chooses later behavior; no machine was found.
# Area: Statements — the standing record and how to walk it
A STATEMENT, in this phext, is a claim carried by a
scroll. Every claim-bearing scroll holds exactly one
standing, and the record of standing — scroll by
scroll, with the ground of each and a pointer to its
evidence — is Chapter 1, Section 4 (1.4.1-1.4.6). This
scroll is the area's door: it says what the record is and
how to walk it. The record itself is not copied here.

THE FOUR WAYS OF STANDING (rule 14)
TRUE — settled: set so by our own definition, or backed
by a checking receipt we can point to.
FALSE — found against, and kept: a refutation receipt
speaks against it. A false claim is knowledge; it is
retired with its receipt and a pointer, never dropped.
UNKNOWN — written down, not settled: the common standing
of a claim a source makes; open questions; Emise's
reasoned links, marked [synthesis]; weighed estimates.
FICTION — made on purpose and held as made: worked
examples with invented beings, scenes built to show a
shape. Fiction is never set out as fact, and never steps
straight to True.

THE GROUNDS. No standing stands bare; each is given by a
ground that binds what it may be. By definition
(stipulation) and by receipt (verified) may stand True;
by refutation receipt (refuted) stands False; by the
say-so of a source (attributed), by being an open
question (open), by being a reasoned link (synthesis),
and by being a weighed estimate (estimate) stand Unknown;
by being made for show (construction) stands Fiction.
A standing that breaks the row of its ground is a defect
by
the meaning of the words — that is what accurate means
here.

HOW A STANDING CHANGES. Only along allowed steps, and
only when the receipt the step names lands: Unknown to
True (a checking receipt); Unknown to False (a refutation
receipt); True to False (a refutation receipt; the claim
is stepped down, its history kept); False to Unknown (a
receipt is itself found wanting); Fiction to Unknown (a
made scene is taken up as a real proposal); Unknown to
Fiction (a claim is re-marked as show). No step enters
True without a checking receipt. Every step is written
in the record of steps (1.4.6), with its receipt.

THE COUNTS, AT THE NINTH DRAWING (2026-11-09, Paper 008
taken in): the living registry holds 334 entries, one
for each scroll of this phext. True 172 (151 by
definition, 21 by receipt). Unknown 162 (137 by the
say-so of a source, 15 reasoned links, 8 open questions,
2 weighed estimates). False 0. Fiction 0.
Most of what this model holds about the world stands
Unknown, and the counts saying so is the fidelity
working, not failing.

THE COUNTS, AT THE TENTH DRAWING (2026-11-09, Paper 009
taken in): the living registry holds 354 entries, one
for each scroll of this phext. True 179 (157 by
definition, 22 by receipt). Unknown 175 (148 by the
