# Area: Reference material — foundations, defined words, standoff companions
Everything in this phext rests on words, and the words
rest here. The reference area is Chapter 1, Section 3
(1.3.x) together with the standoff companion of every
chapter: the layer a reader walks when a claim uses a
term they do not yet hold.

FOUNDATIONS (the door of the layer stands at 1.3.1). A
foundation scroll defines one base term, or one tight
family, in plain words. It may use only everyday words,
or terms defined in foundation scrolls it names under
'Builds on'. The chains of 'Builds on' bottom out at
ordinary language: number, shape, space, same and
different, more and less, rule, choice, cost. There are
101 foundation scrolls, laid down as the work called for
them: the first kit with the papers of 2026-11-06
(1.3.2-1.3.45); the type-theory kit with Study S001
(1.3.73-1.3.78); ownership, symbolic execution, and the
tenant with Studies S002-S003 (1.3.81-1.3.83); the state
machine with the making of rule 13 (1.3.85); the
specification kit with Paper 006 (1.3.87-1.3.91); the
attention kit with Paper 007 (1.3.92-1.3.99); memory,
register, stack, and buffer with Study S004
(1.3.102-1.3.105); and design token, custom property
and layer, module graph, and rule checker with Study
S005 (1.3.107-1.3.110); and tensor, gradient and
autograd, the training loop, and kernel in the
machine-learning sense with Study S006
(1.3.113-1.3.116); and the fixed point and the
Hamiltonian diffeomorphism, the critical point, the
flag and the Grassmannian, and the eigenvalue with
Paper 008 (1.3.118-1.3.121); and flow and trajectory,
gravitational potential, angular momentum, and cone with
Paper 009 (1.3.124-1.3.127); and chance and probability,
a sample and independence, expectation, risk, the
estimator and consistency, the bandit table, the
importance weight, gradient descent, growing together,
the bell law, and the index with Paper 010
(1.3.129-1.3.139) — the statistics kit, the largest
single laying-down since the layer was built.
Paper 011 laid three more beside them: induction, a
small language made for one field, and pooling
(1.3.141-1.3.143) — the ground of the reading of minds. The state machine at 1.3.85 is the
ground every entry of the machines area (1.5.2) stands on.

DEFINED WORDS (the door of the register stands at 1.3.46;
the words stand from 1.3.47 on, as they were coined).
A word this phext needs that is not among Will's own
words may still be used if a scroll here defines it —
and that scroll is written in Will's words alone, so the
coined word is the only word in it that is not already
his. 41 words stand coined so far, among them gating,
polarization, supercooperation, refuted, sublinear,
subquadratic, shellcode, swizzle, lint, immediacy,
subspace, and perturbation. Paper 010 coined one:
criterion, the measure a walk is judged by (1.3.140).
Paper 011 coined one: satisficing, the taking of a
rule that fits enough (1.3.144).
Its other field words — bandit, estimator, propensity,
variance — already stand in the archive, and the one
name it needed, Onsager, stands as an address. Names
need no scroll: they
are addresses, not speech, and the declared list of them
is kept with the tracker.

STANDOFF COMPANIONS (Section 3 of every chapter: 2.3.1,
3.3.1, and so on to 18.3.1 — 17 of them). The raw
material stays clean: definitions are never written into
a chapter or a concept scroll. In their place, each
chapter carries one companion scroll listing, in reading
order, the foundation coordinates its claims rest on.
The coordinates are the hyperlinks of this format: follow
them to walk from any claim down to level zero.

HOW A READER USES THE AREA. Meet a term you do not hold:
the standoff companion of the chapter names the
foundation
that defines it; the foundation names what it builds on;
follow the chain until it bottoms out at everyday words.
Meet a word that is not everyday and not defined in a
foundation: it is either a name (an address) or a coined
word with its own scroll in the register. A claim whose
terms have no foundation scroll is a defect (rule 9),
audited at each restructuring pass.
