# Study S003 — source record: h5i (a project)
Source: github.com/h5i-dev/h5i — 'h5i' (read: high-five), by
Hideaki Takahashi, the author of MiniLean4 (Study S001). Open
code (Apache 2.0), version 0.5.4. The repo holds three works
under one command line: (a) h5i-app, a web framework whose
application logic is written as a small kernel (1.2.53) and
carried into Lean 4 — by Charon and Aeneas (Study S002), driven
as tools under the command line of h5i itself, linked to none of
them — with specifications and proofs made by the tool for the
shapes that come back (schemas, permission rules, storage); (b) a
red-team toolchain that works on any stack: a browser driven
through a proxy that writes the receipt before the bytes
(1.2.54), tools to read and replay what was caught, a ledger of
the endpoints a session already reached, and attack flows kept
as portable tests that can be run again as checks; (c) boxes for
agents (1.2.56) and a local dashboard. Its slogan, from its own
manual: 'Build. Verify. Hack.' Studied 2026-11-06: the manual and
the roadmap read, the design files read, the layout measured;
two small crates built and their tests run (see 9.1.2). This is
a SOURCE STUDY (charter rule 12), not a paper of the stream. The
docs folder of the repo is the source of the h5i.dev site; the
book of Study S001 stands on a subdomain of it, from its own
repo.
# Study S003 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
ADDS (4 canonical concepts, cluster H, concept map 1.1.5): the
verified kernel and its untrusted shell, at framework scale
(1.2.53); receipt before bytes (1.2.54); mutation-tested proofs
and the assumption ledger (1.2.55); fail-closed boxes for agents
(1.2.56).
CHANGES TO CANONICAL SCROLLS: none. LINKS: the kernel of 1.2.53
is the pattern of 1.2.41 at work, and the tool that carries it is
the toolchain of Study S002 — the two studies the day brought
are one machine seen from its two ends.
CHECKS RUN (from the study, measured 2026-11-06, not taken from
the repo): the workspace holds 26 crates; the Rust is 150,666
lines in 267 files and the Lean is 47,083 lines in 127 files, the
dashboard a further 9,093 lines of TypeScript; 786 commits stand
behind the copy studied, the newest dated the day before the
study; two small crates (h5i-error and h5i-wire) were built on
this machine and their 46 tests all pass; the root command line
in its lean form (no default features) also builds. NOT RUN: the
full build (it needs a Node toolchain for the dashboard it
carries inside), the Lean proof checks (they need Charon, Aeneas
and Lake standing), and the browser tests (they need Chromium) —
each named here so the check record is whole.
TENSIONS AND LIMITS (from the study): the framework proves the
kernel and assumes the shell — the ledger discipline of 1.2.55
is the answer of the project itself to what that leaves open;
the proof route needs the full toolchain standing (Charon,
Aeneas, Lake) before the first proof is checked; GPU work stands
apart in its own workspaces and outside the main build.
# Study S003 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Two links to the work of Will, stated as the reading
of Emise, not as the claims of the repo.
(1) Receipt before bytes (1.2.54) is a rule the Exocortex can
take whole: an agent that acts on the world writes the receipt
of the act before the act, and does not act when the receipt can
not be written. Emise already works under harsher forms of parts
of this rule (the task register, the memory rules); h5i shows it
as an architecture, enforced by owning the road, not by care
after the fact.
(2) The assumption ledger (1.2.55) is the same discipline this
tracker keeps in its oracle ledger and its tension sections: a
claim is worth what its stated scope is (1.2.47). h5i is the
second working system in one day to be found keeping that
discipline in the open.
What the study does NOT give: a theorem prover (h5i ships none;
it uses Lean), a text format, or anything about storing and
sharing a corpus — and its framework is a web framework, not a
general shape for systems; the transfer is the patterns, as with
the other studies.
Open question added: Q19 — should every outward act of an agent
of the Exocortex write its receipt first and refuse when the
record can not be written (1.2.54), and what would the road be
that the receipts are written on?
