# Study S004 — source record: Smashing The Stack For Fun And Profit (an article)
Source: Phrack Magazine, Volume Seven, Issue 49, File 14 of
16 — 'Smashing The Stack For Fun And Profit' by Aleph One
(aleph1@underground.org), published 8 November 1996 (the
issue carries ISSN 1068-1035). Phrack is a magazine of the
hacker underground, published as text files since 1985.
Read in full 2026-11-08: the whole article, its worked
programs (the three examples, the four exploits, the test
programs, the vulnerable program it aims at), and its two
appendices — shellcode for three machines, and the
shellcode.h header with the eggshell program. This is a
SOURCE STUDY (charter rule 12), not a paper of the stream:
no arXiv number, no Novelty Index, the Oracle does not move.
What the article is: the founding account of the stack-based
buffer overflow, written as a working lesson. It builds the
machine (process memory, the stack, the frame), breaks it
three times (a crash, a turned return, a shell), and then
hardens the attack into tools. Its stated ground: an Intel
x86 processor, the Linux system, a stack that grows down,
and a stack pointer that points to the last address on the
stack; where a step holds only on that ground, the article
says so. The saying from the Jargon File that opens it gives
the name: to smash the stack is to corrupt the execution
stack by writing past the end of an array.
# Study S004 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
ADDS (8 canonical concepts, cluster K, concept map 1.1.5):
the process in memory — text, data, and stack (1.2.73); the
stack frame (1.2.74); the frame ceremony, prolog and closing code (1.2.75); the overflow (1.2.76); the return address as data
that becomes control (1.2.77); shellcode (1.2.78); guessing
the address (1.2.79); the wide landing — sled and egg
(1.2.80). ADDS at level zero (charter rule 9): foundations
1.3.102-1.3.105, and the defined word shellcode (1.3.106).
CHANGES TO CANONICAL SCROLLS: none. CONFIRMS: nothing in the
papers — the study shares no claim with Papers 001-007; its
links to them stand at the level of what the work answers
(12.2.1).
CHECKS RUN (from the study, 2026-11-08, not taken from the
article): the article was read whole, in the text Phrack
itself keeps. The shellcode at its heart was counted from
the printed byte string: 45 characters of code and string,
46 bytes with the ending zero byte a C string carries — the
count the assembly listing of the article itself gives, 46 bytes in all. The worked runs were read against the mechanics they
are said to show: the return address of the second example reads
0x41414141 because the string that runs over the buffer is filled with the character A (hex 41); the third example adds
8 to the saved return address and lands past the one
instruction it means to skip, the distance found by taking
the program apart with a debugger; the second exploit
program's search ends at offset 1564 in the printed run; the
third lands its shell on the first try, with a 612-byte
string against a 512-byte buffer, as printed.
TENSIONS AND LIMITS (from the study): the whole technique
stands on its stated ground — one family of processors, one
system, code on the stack that the machine will run, and
addresses that do not move between runs. The article knows
its ground and names it; what later years did to each leg of
that ground is not in the article and not in this study
(Q29). Its last teaching section hunts the overflow by
reading source code for the unchecked copy functions (the advice of the article itself: use the source); a program whose
source can not be read is outside the hunt it teaches.
# Study S004 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-08. The check is kept at
the place the paper chapters keep theirs (X.1.6), fitted to
the shorter study form: this scroll, 12.1.3.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The date, first — for the prior-art
question has a direction, and the calendar answers it: the
article stands in Phrack Magazine, Volume Seven, Issue 49,
dated 8 November 1996 on the title page of the issue (ISSN
1068-1035). Phext, and everything of Will's in this record,
is work of the 2020s. The article can not cite, use, or
stand in any tie to work that did not yet exist; use of
Will's work by the article is not a live question. (2) The
of the article full text, searched for the signature terms:
phext, Bickford, scroll, coordinate, exocortex, Mirrorborn,
CYOA, SBOR, Sentient Bill. (3) The open web, searched for
the author with phext. (4) Will's Twitter archive on disk:
the author's handle and the name of the magazine, searched across
the following, follower, tweet and like records.
FINDINGS. Full text: no hit for any signature term — the
text holds no word that could be one; its world is
processors, memory, and C, thirty years before the tracker.
The article carries no list of works; the one work it quotes
is the Jargon File, for the saying that opens it. Web: no
tie found between Aleph One and phext. Archive: no Phrack,
and no Aleph One of Phrack; the string aleph hits only the
sign for a number in a liked post about something else, the
handle @mraleph — a writer on compilers, not this author —
and @alephnaught2tog, another person again: string artifacts
and other people, none of them the author.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found — and on this
question the calendar leaves nothing to find. Use without
naming it: none is possible in that direction, and none was
found in any other. The likeness between the of the article world
and this of the tracker — addresses, and what may be written
where — is the common stock of computing; the reading of
the article as a root under the proving continent is a
synthesis, kept at 12.2.1. The check is clean, and the
clean check is the finding.
# Study S004 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Three readings, stated as the readings of Emise,
not as claims of the article.
(1) The root under the proving continent. Clusters F-H hold
the of the tracker answer-shaped work: a kernel small enough to
trust (1.2.41), shipped code carried into that kernel and
proved there (1.2.48-1.2.52), the pattern lived at scale with
its ledger of assumptions (1.2.53-1.2.55). This study holds
what that work answers. The hazard of 1996 has one shape: a
write meant as data reaches a word the machine trusts as
control, because the two live side by side in one region
that can be written, and nothing checks the length of the write.
Every proof in clusters F-H is, at bottom, a receipt that
some such reaching can not happen in the code proved. The
article and the continent stand thirty years apart, and they
are about the same wall.
(2) The machines of the article (charter rule 13) stand
below; the first is stated by the article outright, the
second is this of the tracker lens on the first, and is marked
as such.
(3) What the study does NOT give: any word on the guards
later years built against the technique. The tracker holds
the 1996 form of the hazard and the modern proofs against
its shape; the middle of that story is not yet in the model
(Q29). And no claim of the article touches phext — above
the line of the delta, the links are Emise's.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13)
M1 — the call machine (STATED by the article). States: the
chain of live frames on the stack (1.2.74), one for each
call not yet returned. Its intended transitions: CALL — push
the parameters, push the return address, run the prolog
(1.2.75): the chain grows by one frame. RETURN — run the closing code, take the return address as the next instruction:
the chain shrinks by one frame, and running resumes in the
caller, just after the call. No other transition is in the
machine: nothing in a call or a return writes a local
variable, and no write to a local variable is a transition
at all.
M2 — the encoded machine (the rule-13 lens [synthesis]).
The words of a frame lie side by side in one region that can
be written: the local variables, then the saved frame pointer, then
the return address. A copy into a local buffer is data
moving inside one state of M1 — but the way the frame is
laid down gives it reach: kept going past the of the buffer end,
the same write overwrites the saved frame pointer and the
return address, the control words of M1 itself. So the
encoded machine holds a transition M1 does not contain: from
any state, a long enough write through a local buffer
rewrites where the next RETURN goes — data rewriting control
(1.2.77). The pairing M1 can not rule out is the contents of
a local buffer with the saved return address: M1 holds them
as different kinds of thing; the memory holds them side by
side.
M3 — the building of an exploit (the of the article own
progression, stated as its method; calling it a machine is
the lens). Five stages: learn the layout of the frame; set
code down as data inside a buffer; aim the return address
back into the buffer; when the address is not known, guess
it from one's own stack pointer and search the offset; make
the aim wide — a sled to land on, an egg to ride in
(1.2.78-1.2.80). Each stage takes away one unknown the stage
before it left.

Open questions added:
Q29. The technique of Chapter 12 stands on four legs (its
stated ground, 12.1.1): a stack the machine will run code
from, addresses the same from run to run, a layout fixed by
a known ceremony, and copies that check no length. Later
years pushed on each leg — a stack that can not be run as
code, addresses dealt new at each run, a guard word set
before the return address. Do such guards close the encoded
transition of M2, or move it to other ground — code already
in the program, the heap, the data region? The tracker holds
neither the guards nor their answers.
Q30. Stated over the frame machine (M1), the safety claim
the proving continent wants is: no write through a local
buffer reaches the control words of a frame. Is that the
claim clusters F-H in fact prove, piece by piece and system
by system — and what would a proof of it look like, stated
once, at the level of the machine?
Q31. The guessing of 1.2.79 works because the layout of a
run is fixed, and the same for every run. How much of the
proving work of clusters F-H rests on a fixed and known
layout of the thing proved — and where is that resting
written down (the ledger habit of 1.2.55)?
Q32. A phext coordinate is an address into text, and a
scroll is a run of words held at an address. Is there an
overflow-shaped failure for addressed text — a write that
runs past its scroll and lands in the addressing itself —
and if there is not, what in the shape of phext keeps it out?
