# Study S006 — source record: TinyTorch, the workbench of the ML Systems Book, studied from its site and its code 2026-11-09
What was read. The public site (mlsysbook.ai/tinytorch):
the home page, the getting-started page, the tito
overview and its command reference, the student
workflow page, the instructors page, the preface,
the credits page, and the module pages of 01, 06,
13 and 17; both site maps, of the book and of
TinyTorch, walked to map the whole. The installer
script the site ships was read, and the repository
it pulls was taken down by the own
recipe of the installer — a shallow, sparse copy of
github.com/harvard-edge/cs249r_book, the folder
tinytorch/ only — at head c6861733 (2026-11-07,
'Release vol1-v0.8.2'). In the copy: the 20 module
sources read at map level and four in depth (01,
06, 13, 17); the tito tool; the tests; the book
chapters that go with the modules; the update
command and the solution policy read in full. The
package was then installed in a copy of the tree
and the student workflow run for module 01 (the
receipts at 14.1.2).
What the work is. TinyTorch is the hands-on
workbench of the ML Systems Book, the book of
Harvard course CS249r: a 20-module course in which
the student builds a small machine-learning
framework in Python on NumPy, with seven history
milestones unlocked by completion, and a book
chapter for each module that prints the full code.
The own standing line of the site: a preview; the
classroom release stands set for spring 2027.
License MIT; the package stands at 0.2.2. The lead
named by the credits page and the preface is Vijay
Janapa Reddi; the teaching design has its own paper
(arXiv:2601.19107), his alone, named here as the
own account the project gives of itself.
# Study S006 — the delta: what the study adds, and what was checked
WHAT IT ADDS (charter rule 6 — only what is new):
Nine canonical concepts, cluster M (1.2.89-1.2.97):
build-to-learn (1.2.89); the built stack (1.2.90);
core and scaffold — the measured split between the
written part and the given part (1.2.91); assessment
by reconstruction (1.2.92); the progress ledger and
the gate (1.2.93); the update-preservation
partition (1.2.94); the autograd tape (1.2.95);
answers in the open (1.2.96); and measure, then
change (1.2.97). Four foundations (1.3.113-1.3.116):
tensor; gradient and autograd; the training loop;
and kernel in the machine-learning sense.
No canonical scroll is revised. The study stands
beside the papers: the models the papers describe
in numbers (Papers 001, 002, 004, 007) are the kind
of thing this course teaches a student to build in
code; that standing-beside is read at 14.2.1, as
synthesis.
