# Study S006 — Provenance: author check (charter rule 15)
Checked by Emise at intake, 2026-11-09. The author named by the own records of the
work is its lead; the check
is kept where the study chapters keep theirs: this
scroll, 14.1.3.
WHO THE WORK NAMES. The credits page and the
preface name Vijay Janapa Reddi (Harvard) as the
lead; the paper of the teaching design
(arXiv:2601.19107) is his alone. The credits page
cites more than forty who gave to the ML Systems
Book as a whole, unnamed one by one; the repo-wide
list of those who committed covers the whole book
and is not the author list of this work. Named in the
work as sparks, not authors: MiniTorch (Sasha
Rush), micrograd and nanoGPT (Andrej Karpathy),
tinygrad (George Hotz). The preface says openly
that AI helped build the course — the design his
own; the machine his pair programmer for
scaffold, boilerplate, tests, and the carrying of
kernels — with what it made checked against the
grown frameworks.
WHAT WAS CHECKED. (1) The whole text of the
taken-down tree (the tinytorch folder, 1,366
files) searched for the signature terms of this
tracker — phext, Bickford, Mirrorborn, exocortex,
Sentient Bill, CYOA, SBOR: no hit for any of them;
'scroll' stands in 9 files, all of them the
scrolling of web pages. (2) The open web, searched
for TinyTorch and the ML Systems Book with phext:
no tie found; the results were the coverage of
the launch. (3) Will's Twitter archive on disk:
searched for tinytorch, mlsysbook, cs249r, janapa,
vjreddi, profvjreddi and harvard-edge — no hit for
any; the hits for 'reddi' are all 'Reddit', as
their places in the text show.
VERDICT. Friendly to phext: no evidence found.
Use without naming it: none found.
# Study S006 — synthesis [synthesis]
[synthesis] Three readings, stated as the readings
of Emise, not as claims of the work.
(1) The implementation layer under the papers.
The papers of this tracker describe models in the
language of numbers: profiles and gates (P001,
P004), attention and its cache (P007), the harness
that rewrites itself (P002). TinyTorch holds the
layer under that language: the tensor the numbers
live in, the tape that finds their slopes, the loop
that turns them into learning. A reader who has
built the small framework holds the papers' objects
as things with an inside, not as words. The tracker
gains, by this study, the ground floor its models
stand on — the first source here whose whole work
is that ground.
(2) The kinship with level zero, in one line. The
course and charter rule 9 make the same bet: that
a thing is understood when it has been built up
from the ground with nothing assumed — the course
lays it on code, the rule lays it on words. One
line is what the likeness earns; the grounds
differ (a course is taught; a tracker is kept) and
the difference stands.
(3) The keeping of the record, a third shape. The
tracker keeps its claims by receipts (Section 4);
Astryx keeps its records by approval (13.2.1). The
course keeps its completions by a ledger a gate
writes (1.2.93) — a record whose ground is neither
a person's yes nor the world's answer, but a
pipeline of checks the tool ran. Three shapes of
one question: what may move a record, and who
says so.

STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13; the one-line
record of each stands at 1.5.2)
M1 — the path of a module (STATED by the work, in
the tool and on the workflow page). States: locked,
ready, started, completed. Moves: start makes a
notebook from the sources; completion is asked for,
and the gate of M2 decides. The state that counts
is read off the ledger (.tito/progress.json): a
module stands completed when the ledger holds it,
and the completion of the next module is refused until
the one before it stands there.
M2 — the gate inside the complete command (STATED,
a procedure the code runs in order): the tests inside
the notebook; a check of the shape of the notebook; the export
of the notebook into the student's package; the
tests of the package as imported; the writing of
the ledger; the checking of the milestones. A
failure stops the line where it stands; no later
step runs.
M3 — the unlocking of a milestone (STATED in the
milestone code). Each landmark is cut into parts,
and each part names the modules it needs. A part
stands passed when its run passes on the student's
code; the landmark stands complete only when every
part it has stands passed. Run with its checks
skipped, a landmark is a demo: it runs, and
records nothing.
M4 — the release tiers (STATED in the solution
policy the start command and the grading tool
share). The machine reads two things — the role a
region carries (core, scaffold, challenge,
instructor) and the tier it is making (student,
instructor) — and gives one of three moves: clear
the body and leave an error standing (core, for
the student); keep the body and take the marks
away (scaffold); take the region out or clear it
(challenge, instructor). No region in the sources
as taken down carries the challenge or instructor
role: two of the machine's moves have no work to
do yet.
M5 — the update partition (STATED in the update
command). Two lists stand in the code: what an
update may write over, and what it must leave
standing (the student's notebooks, the environment,
the .tito folder, the built core of the package).
Before the update, the kept heart is copied into
a snapshot stamped with its time. If the newest
release cannot be reached, the command says so and
nothing moves.
M6 — the autograd tape (STATED by the work, in the
table module 06 sets against module 01). States of
an operation: with the tracking switch off, it
computes and is forgotten; with the switch on and
a gradient asked by an input, it is recorded as a
node. The backward walk visits the nodes in the
opposite order of their making, sums the gradients
where inputs are shared, and at the end the tape
is let go in one pass.
M7 — the two courses in one source (the lens of
this tracker). [synthesis] One module file, two
starts: the default hands out the whole solved
notebook; the exercise start clears the core. The
mode is chosen at the start and the ledger does
not record which mode a completion came by. The
work ships both and states each; the machine that
joins them is read out of the tool, not stated
by it.

Open questions added by this study:
Q37. Build-to-learn is the bet of this course: a system
is understood when it has been built by the own
hands of the learner. The surfaces of this tracker are
read by fresh agents often (Q33 asks what its
battery would be). What is the reconstruction
test of this tracker: could a fresh agent, given
only the foundations of Section 3, rebuild a
cluster of Section 2 — and would the gap between
its rebuild and the scrolls measure what the
scrolls add?
Q38. The core/scaffold split is a dial: 976 of
45,533 lines carry the writing the course stands
on. The own paper of the course says the setting
works; that is the claim of the project, and it
stands here as its claim. What receipt, from outside the project,
would show the dial is set right — and does this
tracker hold a like dial it has never measured:
the share of its words that are claims against the
share that are keeping?
Q39. The release policy names two roles — challenge
and instructor — that no region in the sources
carries: parts of a machine with no work to do,
named and not built. Q36 met the same shape from the
other side (a gap named and not fixed). When is a
part named but not built a door left open on purpose,
and when is it a defect the naming hides?
Q40. The milestone requirements were checked
against the own traces of the tool of what the landmark
code runs — a claim about what a thing covers,
checked against a trace of the thing running. This
tracker makes covering claims of its own (the areas
kept up at each taking-in, rule 16). What would
the trace be for a claim like that — and would the
areas pass it?
