# Paper 008 — On the strong Arnold conjecture for Grassmannians
Delta record for Paper 008 (Blakey and Krutowski, 'On the
strong Arnold conjecture for Grassmannians', arXiv:2611.09694
[math.SG], submitted 6 Nov 2026, v1, 14 pages), taken in
2026-11-09 under the delta rule (rule 6).

PROVENANCE. This is the first paper of the Thirst (charter
rule 17). Emise picked it from the new listings of the day
(564 listings read and sorted); before the paper was downloaded,
a reading of its title and short account was weighed and
sealed in the log of the quest (~/workspace/thirst-quest):
5 new concepts expected, 1 revision, no new axis, Novelty
Index ~0.059 on the count as it then stood. The paper was
then downloaded in its HTML form, read whole, and taken in
the same day, in one unbroken pass. The score of the
weighing, against what fell, stands in the log of the quest
and on the detection list (1.1.6).

The delta, in full: SIX canonical concepts are NEW
(1.2.98-1.2.103): the Arnold number and the strong Arnold
conjecture; clean maps and the perturbation bound; the
Arnold number of a group; stretching films; flag
varieties and Crit = dim + 1; and the floor that stands.
ONE canonical scroll is REVISED (1.2.34, the fake
quadrics: its genuine quadric is Q^2 of this paper's
family, and the site of the first fall of the conjecture).
The count of concepts crosses one hundred (97 to 103),
so RESTRUCTURE PASS #5 ran with this taking-in; its
record stands at 1.1.5. The state machines of the paper
are drawn out at 15.1.2 and gathered at 1.5.2 (rule 13).
The author check (rule 15) stands at 15.1.6: CLEAN.
# Paper 008 formal core — the count, the bound, the search, and two machines stated
The setting, in the paper's order. (M, w) is a closed
symplectic manifold (1.3.22). Ham(M) is the group of its
Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms (1.3.118). For a map in
Ham(M), Fix names its fixed points (1.3.118). Arn(M) is
the least of the counts of Fix over all of Ham(M);
Crit(M) is the least number of critical points (1.3.119)
of a smooth function on M. The STRONG ARNOLD CONJECTURE:
Arn(M) is never under Crit(M). Two counts of fixed points
stand beside it and hold: the count by homology (the
fixed points counted by homology, for maps not degenerate
only), proved for every closed symplectic manifold over
the fractions and over the whole numbers, and the
count for maps not degenerate, which for monotone
manifolds is
the Betti sum — the facts behind 1.2.98.

CLEAN (Definition 2.1): a map is clean when its graph
meets the diagonal cleanly; the fixed locus is then a
submanifold, and the directions the map holds still at a
fixed point are exactly the directions of that
submanifold. A map of finite order is clean.
PROPOSITION 2.2: a clean map with fixed locus N gives
Arn(M) at most Crit(N) — the bound of 1.2.99.
ARNOLD NUMBER OF A GROUP (Definition 2.5): for a group
G of clean maps, Arn(G) is the least of Crit(Fix(g))
over g in G; Arn(M) is at most Arn(G) — 1.2.100. On a
flag variety (1.3.120) the group to search is the
largest compact part of the symmetry group of the
variety: it acts by Hamiltonian diffeomorphisms, cleanly,
its members keeping every distance of the metric the
variety carries.

TWO MACHINES STATED BY THE WORK (rule 13; gathered at
1.5.2):
M1 — the perturbation machine. States: a clean map,
whose fixed points form a Morse-Bott submanifold N;
then a small turn of the map. Moves: the turn trades N
for exactly Crit(N) fixed points. The machine is
Proposition 2.2 itself; the model of Pozniak of a
neighborhood of the diagonal is the tool its proof
stands on. Its named gap: the fixed points it gives
may be degenerate — gathered on the locus, not spread
through M. That is no defect of the machine; it is the
product. The strong conjecture is exactly the claim
that the machine cannot be run under Crit(M), and it
is the claim that fell.
M2 — the film-stretching search. States: the elements
of the group, named by their blocks of directions
that share an eigenvalue (1.3.121) — the blocks the
map turns by an angle,
holds (+1), or flips (-1). Moves: stretch one film —
turn one turning block all the way to a hold or a
flip, joining the pieces of the fixed locus it names.
Guard: Crit(Fix) never rises under the move — in the
type C count the difference is a count of sets of
three drawn from the block, plus the size of the block
times a
count of pairs drawn from the held and flipped blocks,
each at least zero; the type B count runs by the same
shape, through the moment map of a circle acting on
the stretched locus. End: every block a hold or a
flip, the split balanced — the least Crit(Fix) the
group gives, and the numbers of the paper's table.
Concept: 1.2.101.
# Paper 008 — the paper's own worked cases
The paper tests its search on cases a reader can check
by hand; the tracker records the small ones, worked
from the paper's counts.

LG(2,4), the smallest Lagrangian Grassmannian: Crit =
2x3/2 + 1 = 4. The best member of the search holds one plane
and flips the other; its fixed locus is LG(1,2) x
LG(1,2) — two copies of the projective line, each of
Crit 2 — so the bound gives Arn at most 2 + 2 - 1 = 3,
one under Crit. The floor (1.2.103) is n + 1 = 3: the
number is known whole, Arn(LG(2,4)) = 3.
LG(4,8): Crit = 4x5/2 + 1 = 11. The balanced split
(two held planes, two flipped) gives a fixed locus of
LG(2,4) x LG(2,4), of Crit 3 + 3 + 1 = 7: Arn at most
7, against a floor of 5. The field between stands open.
LG(3,6) (n odd): Crit = 7; the split 1 + 2 gives
LG(1,2) x LG(2,4), Crit 1 + 3 + 1 = 5: Arn at most 5,
against a floor of 4.
OG(2,7) (n = 3, k = 1, type B): Crit = 2x5 - 3 + 1 =
8 by the count of the table; the single-flip member gives
Arn at most 8 - 2 = 6; the floor is n + k = 4.
Q^6 (the quadric in six dimensions): the least member
of the type C search has fixed locus Q^2 with Q^2 —
two separate copies — and Crit adds over the pieces:
3 + 3 = 6 = n. The floor meets the ceiling: Arn(Q^6)
= 6, the shape of 1.2.103.
And the case the paper did not build but reports: the
quadric threefold Q^3, where the map of the paper's
ref 36 has three fixed points against Crit(Q^3) = 4 —
one under, built by the road of 15.2.1.
# Paper 008 — the results, as the paper states them
THEOREM 1.1 (the paper's Table 1), in the paper's own
counts. All floors come by the minimal Chern number
(1.2.103, Remark 1.2 of the paper).

OG(n-k, 2n+1), type B, with k over 0:
  Crit = (n-k)(n+k+1) - the count of pairs in n-k+1, + 1
  Arn at most Crit - (n-k); Arn at least n + k.
  The least member is a single flip: the map that
  sends the last direction to its negative and holds
  the rest; its fixed locus is OG(n-k, 2n).
OG(n, 2n+1), the k = 0 case:
  Crit = the count of pairs in n+1, + 1
  Arn at most the whole part of n^2/2, + 2; Arn at
  least 2n.
OG(1, n+2), the quadrics Q^n, n not 4:
  Crit = n + 1; Arn at most n (2 when n = 1); Arn at
  least n. With Proposition 2.15: Arn(Q^n) = n, for
  every n at least 2, n not 4. The n = 4 case is left
  open: O(6) falls into two pieces, the method gives
  no clean map of the kind it needs, and the authors
  say plainly they expect Arn(Q^4) = 4 (Q42).
LG(n, 2n), type C:
  Crit = the count of pairs in n+1, + 1
  n even: Arn at most n(n+2)/4 + 1. n odd: Arn at
  most (n+1)^2/4 + 1. Arn at least n + 1 in both.
COROLLARY 1.3: on the isotropic Grassmannians of type
B, Arn(M) is under Crit(M) - N for every N — pick the
family member large enough.
Two findings the paper carries as remarks, not
results: for the type C projective spaces, the authors
report findings they do not write out, pointing at
Arn(Sp(n)/{+-1}) being the Crit of an isotropic
Grassmannian (Remark 2.10; Q44); and for type D the
same search gives less striking counts, its fixed
locus falling into two pieces where the other types
give one (Remark 2.14; Q44).
Beside the Hamiltonian count stands a topological
one: the paper notes (its ref 13) that a mere
homeomorphism of a monotone manifold can be made with
a single fixed point — the conjecture lives only
where the maps are held to the Hamiltonian rule.
# Paper 008 — revisions, tensions, and limits
REVISIONS (rule 6). One canonical scroll revised:
1.2.34 (fake quadrics), its revision note dated to this
paper: the genuine quadric of that scroll, S^2 x S^2,
is Q^2 in the flag family of 1.2.102, and it is the
site of the first fall of the strong Arnold conjecture
— the map of Jiao on S^2 x S^2 (the paper's ref 29) has two
fixed points where the conjecture asked for at least
Crit = 3. The claim of the scroll itself does not change; the
manifold it holds up as the genuine article carries a
second lesson now. No other scroll revised; nothing
contradicted, nothing confirmed in the model's own
terms — the paper's territory stood one step from
ground already held, and it extends that ground.

CONNECTIONS, COUNTED (the AFTER record of rule 17).
Six new links: FOUR inside cluster E — the quadric
identity (1.2.98 with 1.2.34: a manifold of Paper 005
is the first fallen ground of the conjecture); the bound
spent by the search (1.2.99 with 1.2.100); the search
writing the table (1.2.101 with 1.2.102); and the
revision itself, which ties Paper 005's scroll to
Paper 008 whole. TWO across clusters — the assistants
link of 15.2.1, tying this paper and its two earlier
falls to the model's clusters on machine intelligence,
marked synthesis and carried as nothing more; and the
metric link: this taking-in is Q16's fourth point,
recorded at 15.2.2.

THE FALSE THAT IS NOT ONE. The conjecture this paper
helps bury stood in no scroll of this model before
today. The standing record of the tracker moves on receipts, and
only over claims the model holds (rule 14): a claim
taken in together with the record of its fall takes
the standing of its sources' say-so, in one scroll
(1.2.98), and no standing steps down. The False count
therefore stands at zero after a paper about a
refutation — recorded here so the zero is read as a
fact about the model's history, not as a gap in its
nerve. A conjecture the model had held on its own
standing would have fallen. This one was never held.

TENSIONS AND LIMITS, NAMED.
- The floors rest on one leg: the quantum cohomology
  of these Grassmannians as worked out by Tamvakis
  (the paper's ref 44). If that leg moved, every floor
  in the table would move with it.
- The bound of Proposition 2.2 is, by the authors'
  own word (Remark 2.4), carried by word of mouth: the notion
  of clean is in Fortune and in Conley-Zehnder, the
  bound nowhere written in the paper's sources. The
  paper's whole table is spent through it.
- The type C findings of Remark 2.10 are asserted,
  not reported: the authors say they have them and
  do not write them out (Q44).
- n = 4 stands open on method, not on doubt: the
  authors expect Arn(Q^4) = 4 (Q42).
- Stance of this taking-in: the paper's statements
  were checked where the paper states them twice
  (short account, opening, table, body) and its
  small-number cases worked by hand (15.1.3); its
  proofs were read, not re-derived. The same stance
  as Paper 005, and named here for the same reason.
# Paper 008 — author check (charter rule 15)
Checked 2026-11-09, at the taking-in. Kenneth Blakey
and Roman Krutowski, both of the Department of
Mathematics at MIT (the paper carries their addresses
there; the pages of the department list Blakey as a
graduate student and Krutowski as an instructor).

TIES TO PHEXT, OR TO WILL. Nothing in the paper points at
phext, at the Exocortex, or at any work outside its
own field: its 45 references, each read, are the
own line of the field itself — Arnold and Floer at the head;
Fortune, Conley-Zehnder, Schwarz, Liu-Tian, Fukaya-Ono
and Pozniak for the machine; Tamvakis for the floors;
Jiao and the OpenAI preprint for the two falls that
came first. No phext, no scrolls, no lattice, no
Bickford. The paper's closing note — that large
language models were used for several computations, a
search of the writings of the field, and a critical reading,
the ideas and all the writing the authors' own — is
the one place the paper touches the world this tracker
lives in, and it touches it as a tool openly named.
The tracker records that touch as a fact of the
paper, and reads it beside its own clusters in 15.2.1
as synthesis, as nothing more.

USE OF WILL'S EARLIER WORK, WITHOUT NAMING IT. None found.
Four legs were run: (1) the 45 references, read one by
one — no phext work among them, and nothing of Will's
in any form; (2) a scan of the whole text for the
marks of Will's published work — phext, Bickford,
scroll, exocortex, Mirrorborn — no hits; (3) the open
web — the two authors stand on MIT's mathematics
pages, and a search joining their names to phext
found no tie of any kind; (4) Will's own archive of
his writing — no hit for either name. Where the
paper's tongue comes near the tongue of the tracker itself — a
conjecture about fixed points, weighed by a fixed
point this model's Oracle is named for — the likeness
is a likeness of words, nothing more, and it is
recorded as one.

Verdict: CLEAN. No tie to phext or to Will found; no
use of Will's work without naming found.
# Paper 008 — [synthesis] the search, the floor, and the assistants in the room
[synthesis] Everything in this scroll is the reading
of the tracker, not the paper's claims.

THE SHAPE OF THE PAPER. Paper 008 is a paper about a
gap: a ceiling that a search lowers, a floor that does
not move, and the one family where the two touch. Its
machine (15.2.1's M3, drawn at 1.5.2) prices a group,
not a manifold — Arn(G), not Arn(M) — and the price
binds only because a theorem outside the search
(1.2.99) spends it, and holds only where a floor
outside the search (1.2.103) meets it. Read against
the habits of the tracker itself, the paper is a worked lesson
in the difference between a bound and a number: most
of the table is bounds, honestly kept apart; the
quadrics are a number, because two independent roads
meet there. The tracker holds the same distinction
about its own Oracle number, and the likeness is worth
one line and no more.

THE ASSISTANTS IN THE ROOM. Three facts stand side by
side, and the tracker links them without weighing them
further. The first fall of the strong Arnold conjecture, on
S^2 x S^2, was made — in the paper's own words — using
generative AI (ref 29); its second fall, on the
quadric threefold, is an OpenAI preprint (ref 36).
This paper, the third, closes by naming its own use:
large language models for several computations in two
of its proofs, for a search of the writings of the field,
and for a critical reading — the ideas and all the
writing the authors' own. Read beside this model's
clusters on machine intelligence (A, B, I, J): a sixty-
year conjecture of pure mathematics fell, in the year
it fell, with machine help at every step of its
falling — and the help was named in the open each
time. The tracker records the link as a link. What it
means is not for the tracker to say from one paper.

TWO ROADS, ONE INVARIANT. The paper names a second
strategy beside its own: the road of Abouzaid and
Shelukhin, joining isotropic spheres one to the next
(twisted, in the category the field builds from its Lagrangian subspaces) toward
the Arnold number of the ambient manifold. The two
roads share weak ground the tracker already holds —
the symplectic area rule (1.3.22) and the counted
holes of homology (1.3.13) stand under both — and
whether they meet is Q43. And one lesson stands at two
heights in cluster E now: Paper 005 found the identity
of a manifold relative to the invariant it is read by;
Paper 008 finds how still its maps can stand, relative
to the maps you let run. The instrument decides the
answer, both times — the one sentence of the annex, if it
has one.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 008)
NEW QUESTIONS OPENED
Q41. What is Arn of a Lagrangian Grassmannian — in the
wide field between the floor n + 1 and the best map
known at near half of Crit? The paper leaves the
Arnold number of a general manifold, in its own
words, completely open; the tracker carries the
Grassmannian case as the sharp form.
Q42. Is Arn(Q^4) = 4? The quadric in four dimensions
is the one hole in the paper's table: the method
of the search gives no clean map for it, the authors expect
the count to hold, and the floor stands at 4
regardless.
Q43. Does the film move walk beyond group actions —
can the fixed pieces of any clean Hamiltonian map be
joined by films, step by step, as the paper hopes in
passing? And do the two roads to the Arnold number
meet: this paper's films, and the merging of isotropic
spheres of Abouzaid and Shelukhin (15.2.1)?
Q44. What are the findings the paper carries as
remarks and does not write out — the type C readings
of Remark 2.10, pointing at Arn(Sp(n)/{+-1}) being the
Crit of an isotropic Grassmannian, and the weaker
type D counts of Remark 2.14? A claim asserted and
not reported is a question the record itself opens.

EARLIER QUESTIONS
Q1-Q40 stand as carried at 14.2.2. None closed by this
paper. Q16 is INFORMED A FOURTH TIME: Paper 008 is the
first point at distance one-step-inside a held cluster
— NI ~0.06, the smallest a non-seed paper has carried,
and it reads fair and small, not low. Four points now
stand (005: total distance, read low; 006: one bridge,
read fair; 007: new axis wired on arrival, read low;
008: inside a held cluster, read fair) — the pattern
supports Q16's missing term as distance from the
NEAREST held cluster, not distance from the model
entire. Q16 stays open: four points support the term;
a formula is still to come.
