# Paper 009 — delta record: Singularities and Collision Criteria in the Gravitational Straight-Segment Problem with Arbitrary Positive Density

Source: arXiv:2611.08625 (v1), F. Crespo, E. Martínez,
J. Vidarte, J.L. Zapata, A. Abdrabou — a standing line of
celestial mechanics between Chile and Florida: Crespo of
Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University, Vidarte of the
Universidad Católica de la Santísima Concepción, Zapata of
the Universidad San Sebastián, as their joint work prints
them; Martínez, whose 2025 thesis at the Universidad del
Bío-Bío (the paper's reference [13]) is the root this paper
grows from. Mathematics Subject Classification 70F15,
37N05, 70F16. Support named in the paper: ANID–FONDECYT
Iniciación grants 11240582 (Vidarte) and 11250703 (Zapata).
Read in full, in its HTML form, 2026-11-09.

THE THIRST (charter rule 17): the second paper of the
quest, and the first the quest did not pick — Will posted
this one into the side channel himself, hunting alongside
the daily scan. It was weighed blind all the same, from its
title and abstract alone, and the weighing sealed in the
log of the quest (commit da13559) before the paper was
read: 6 new concepts, 1 revision, an axis opened, Index
~0.06, 3 links, 2 questions, the Oracle to ~42% (33-52).
The score stands at 16.1.5 and in the log of the quest.

THE DELTA, IN ONE PARAGRAPH: a new field, entire — the
first classical mechanics the tracker holds. A particle with no mass of its own
moves in the pull of a fixed segment carrying any
positive density; the paper answers where the road of the particle
can end. Every finite-time singularity of the flow
forces the particle to the segment (1.2.106); every
approach has zero angular momentum about the axis
of the segment, and so lies in one plane through it (1.2.107); every approach in the
plane is a collision, or, read backward, an
ejection (1.2.108). And a test with a clock: an angular
cone on position and momentum that, once entered with
inward momentum, promises collision within a time read off
the start (1.2.109) — aimed, in the paper's own framing, at
spacecraft working near long thin asteroids. Six new
canonical concepts, one new cluster (N), no canonical
scroll revised: the Hamiltonian ground the symplectic wing
stands on held as laid, and the new field hangs from it by
that one bridge.
# Paper 009 — formal core, as stated by the paper

The setting (§2 of the paper). The segment S is the part
of the x-axis from −L to L. The density σ is any rule for
mass per length on S, continuous, held between two positive
bounds: 0 < σ_min ≤ σ(x) ≤ σ_max. The potential is
U(q) = −G ∫ σ(x)/|q − x| dx over the segment (the
foundation at 1.3.125), and the motion is the Hamiltonian
flow of H(q, p) = ½|p|² + U(q) on the states outside S.
Remark 2.1: U at q is set by the two end-distances r1, r2
alone. Remark 2.2: at negative energy the position stays in
one bounded part of space — the road cannot leave for
infinity. The turn-symmetry about the x-axis keeps
ℓ = p_φ fixed; in the fitted coordinates (ξ along the
axis, ρ out from it, φ around it) the energy reads
H = ½(p_ξ² + p_ρ² + ℓ²/ρ²) + U(ξ, ρ).

The results (§§3-5), in the order the paper spends them:
Lemma 3.2 — the log sandwich on |U| (concept 1.2.105).
Theorem 3.3 — every finite-time singularity has δ → 0
(concept 1.2.106). Theorem 4.1 — an approach has ℓ = 0,
hence lies in a fixed plane through S (concept 1.2.107).
Theorem 4.4 — an approach in the plane arrives at a point of S;
Corollary 4.5 — every singularity is a collision forward,
an ejection backward (concept 1.2.108). Definition 5.1 —
the angular cone C(q*); Lemma 5.2 — the cone is forward
invariant; Theorem 5.3 — in the cone with p_ρ0 < 0,
collision by ω+ ≤ −ρ0/p_ρ0; Remark 5.4 — the p_ρ0 = 0 case
by a time-shift; Theorem 5.5 — the final arc of every
interior collision lies in the cone; §5.1 with Proposition
5.6 — a sharper, bending clock under an added condition on
the acceleration, shown for constant density (concept
1.2.109).

THE STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13), as the paper states
them:
M1 — the flow and its endings. States: the states outside
S, each carrying its kept energy and its ℓ. Moves:
the Hamiltonian flow, for as long as the road runs. Ends:
the run of a road can stop at a finite time only in the approach
state (δ → 0, Theorem 3.3), from which the ℓ-gate (Theorem
4.1) passes only ℓ = 0 roads into the plane, and the
roads in the plane land (Theorem 4.4). The forbidden state — a
finite end with the particle standing clear of the body —
is the machine's whole content: the paper proves that state
cannot be reached. Guard: at negative energy, escape to
infinity is likewise closed (Remark 2.2).
M2 — the cone trap. States: a state is in the cone C(q*)
or out of it (Definition 5.1). Moves: the flow in the plane.
Once in, never out: the sight-point of the momentum is drawn to
the average sight-point of the segment and stays in its
reach, and the position stays in the triangle (Lemma 5.2).
Guard: inward momentum, p_ρ < 0 (or the time-shift of
Remark 5.4). Exit: only through the floor — collision, by
the clock of Theorem 5.3. Gap, in the paper's own shape:
the trap certifies the roads it holds; it does not say the
roads outside it are safe — one outside road of the paper's
own numerical runs goes clear (§6, case (d)), and no theorem here
closes the outside.
# Paper 009 — the paper's numerical cases, as reported

The paper's §6 runs the theory against four densities on
one segment — constant; straight-line (σ = 1 + x/4);
waved (a sine wave along the segment); center-heavy (σ = 2 − x²) —
all with L = 1 and the constants of the pull set to 1. The straight clock of Theorem 5.3
stands at 2 for the starts with ρ0 = 1, p_ρ0 = −0.50, and
at 1.6 for case (c) (ρ0 = 0.8). Every time printed is a
near-collision time: the running stops at a small distance
from the segment, not on it.

(a) In the cone, inside the reach of the segment: collision,
times 0.814–0.945 across the four densities — all far under
the bound, as Theorem 5.3 promises.
(b) On the edge of the cone, at the plane through an endpoint:
collision, 0.833–0.905.
(c) In the cone but starting outside the span of the segment
along the axis — the start (2.2, 0.8, 0), beyond the end:
collision, 0.890–0.944. The cone reaches past the body it
guards.
(d) In the plane (ℓ = 0) but outside the cone: no collision
in the window of the run [0, 12]; the particle moves away from
the segment instead. The plane is necessary for arrival,
not sufficient — Theorem 4.1 read from the other side.
(e) ℓ = 0.30, not zero: collision barred by Theorem 4.1
itself; the numerical runs only confirm what the theorem already
closed.
And the wide check: 1,000 starts sampled in the cone, under
the constant density: every collision time fell under
both clocks — the straight bound, and the sharper bending
clock of §5.1, whose condition the paper proves for that
density (Proposition 5.6).
# Paper 009 — results as stated

What the paper claims, in its own reach. The theorems hold
for every continuous density bounded away from zero and
from above on the segment — no demand that the density be even, no straight-line
growth, no special shape. The plane of the motion is not
assumed: earlier work in this line (the Alberti–Vidal
papers the paper cites) studied an constant density
inside a fixed symmetry plane; the root thesis [13] lifted
the density to straight-line growth; this paper takes the
density general and lets the plane fall out of the approach
itself.

What is proved, and what is only run. Proved: the log
sandwich and the approach theorem (§3); the ℓ-gate, the
plane, arrival, and the classification (§4); the invariance
of the cone, the straight clock, and the final-arc portrait
(§5); the bending clock under its added condition, the
condition itself shown for constant density (§5.1,
Proposition 5.6). Run, not proved: the four densities of
§6 and the 1,000 sampled starts — numerical runs in the paper's
own framing, printed as near-collision times. Not claimed:
any word on roads that never approach (they may run
forever, in or out of the plane); any word on a segment
that itself moves or turns — the rotating segment of the cited line
[12] is the ground of another entry; the bending clock
for a general density (Q47).
# Paper 009 — revisions, tensions, limits

REVISIONS OF HELD GROUND: none. The weighing had expected
one revision, and named its ground in advance only by
shape — the Hamiltonian floor the new field shares with
the symplectic wing. Read whole, the paper stands on that
floor exactly as laid: the flow of 1.3.118 is its engine,
the symplectic ground of 1.3.22 its stage, and nothing in
the claim of either scroll needs a changed word. The new field
hangs from the held ground; it does not move it. The
revision count is zero, and the miss stands against the skim (below).

TENSIONS AND LIMITS. The plane is proved only at the end
of an approaching road; the paper is silent, by design, on
the long run of roads that never approach. The cone
certifies inward starts; its outside has no map — the
paper's case (d) shows one outside road running clear, and
no result here says whether outside roads can also arrive
(Q46). The final-arc portrait (Theorem 5.5) is drawn for
collisions at inner points of the segment; the endpoints —
where the triangle of the cone falls flat — are outside its
reach (Q45). The bending clock is conditional beyond the
constant density (Q47). The applied tail — spacecraft
near long asteroids — is the paper's stated aim; the
segment is held fixed and the particle has no mass of its own, and the
paper does not price what rotation of the body would do to
the cone.

CONNECTIONS, COUNTED (the count of new links the taking-in
surveyed; the prose of the concepts carries them): 4 new
links. Internal to cluster N: 3 — the log wall under the
approach theorem (1.2.105 → 1.2.106); the log wall against
the barrier, the race the ℓ-gate is won by (1.2.105 →
1.2.107); the clock of the cone spent on the arrival of
the classification (1.2.109 → 1.2.108). Crossing to a held cluster: 1
— the Hamiltonian bridge to cluster E: the flow whose
time-one maps the Arnold concepts count fixed points of
(1.2.98, on the ground of 1.3.118) is the same flow whose
endings this paper classifies; two wings of one annex, one
foundation stone. The synthesis at 16.2.1 reads the cone as
a screening machine for the applied tail; that reading is
a marked synthesis link and is not in this count.

THE SCORE OF THE WEIGHING (charter rule 17; the sealed
weighing stands in the log of the quest, commit da13559;
the concepts stood at 103 when it was sealed and at 103
when the paper was taken in — no drift, one count serves
both). Expected: 6 new, 1 revised, axis yes, Index ~0.0596,
3 links, 2 questions, Oracle ~42% (33-52), hold or slight
fall. As it fell: 6 new, 0 revised, axis yes — a new field
