# Paper 009 — synthesis: the second wing of the annex, and the cone as a screen [synthesis]

[synthesis] Paper 008 closed with two roads to one floor:
the same count reached by machinery that shares no step,
and the floor the one both stop at. Paper 009 is that
shape again, one level down — a classification reached by
a race of growth rates: the log against the square. The
pull of the segment deepens too slowly to pay for turning; so
turning roads cannot arrive, plane roads must, and the
book of endings closes with two entries. The tracker has
now twice watched a hard classification fall out of a
comparison of rates (here; and the barrier shape inside
the floor of 1.2.103) — the shape is worth naming where it
stands: when two rates of growth race and one always wins, whole
families of roads are settled at once, without following
any of them.

[synthesis] M3 — the screening machine (rule 13, inferred;
the paper states the theorems, the machine is this
reading). States: the state of a spacecraft near a long body,
read as (q, p) against the body's segment. Moves: the
checks, in order — ℓ first (not zero: collision is barred
by Theorem 4.1, whatever else is true); then the cone (in
the cone with inward momentum: collision is certain, and
its clock is read off the start, Theorem 5.3); then silence
(outside the cone: this paper gives no verdict — case (d)
ran clear, and silence is not safety). Guards: the body
held still, the craft with no mass of its own, the density positive. Gap:
the screen is one-sided by construction — it can promise a
collision and can bar one, and between those two
it says nothing; a navigator who reads silence as safety
has added a theorem the paper does not hold. The machine is
the applied face the introduction holds out — collision
criteria for spacecraft working close to long asteroids —
and it ties cluster N to the work the tracker serves
[synthesis]: a test that can be checked from a state,
without flying the road.

[synthesis] And the wing itself: the symplectic annex
(cluster E) was built by papers that ask what maps a space
must allow. This wing asks where a road can end. The two
questions share one engine — the Hamiltonian flow — and the
annex now holds both ends of it: the maps the flow makes
(1.2.98), and the endings the flow allows (1.2.106).
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 009)

This paper takes the endings of one flow whole, and opens the
edges of its own results:

Q45. The endpoints. The final-arc portrait (Theorem 5.5)
is drawn for collisions at inner points of the segment; at
the endpoints the triangle of the cone falls flat and the portrait
is not drawn. What does the last arc of an endpoint
collision look like — and does some cone, flattened to the
line, still hold it?
Q46. The outside of the cone. In the cone, collision is
certain; one outside road of the paper's numerical runs ran
clear. Can an outside road arrive at all — is the cone, in
the end, also necessary for the starts it does not hold,
or is its outside genuinely mixed?
Q47. The bending clock for every density. The sharper
collision clock of the paper's §5.1 stands on a condition
on the acceleration, shown for constant density
(Proposition 5.6) and supported by the numerical runs on four
densities. Does the condition hold for every positive
density, or is there a density that breaks it — and with
it, the clock?

Earlier questions this paper informs:
Q16 (the Index needs a connectivity term) — INFORMED, a
fifth time, and never more plainly. Papers 008 and 009
bring the same count of concepts (six) and near the same
Index (0.063 against 0.055) — yet 008 landed inside a held
cluster and revised held ground, while 009 opened a field
the model held nothing of. The Index reads 009 the less
novel of the two. The distance between a paper and the
model, which the Index does not carry, is not a small
correction at these sizes; it is the larger half of the
signal. Q16 stands open.
Q32 (how literally code moves may be read — and what
'failure' could mean for a reading) — INFORMED, lightly:
the flow of M1 at 16.1.2 is a machine whose failure states
are classified whole — every breakdown an approach, every
approach an arrival — the first such classification in the
model, standing as a shape a failure-question can take.
Nothing in the paper answers Q32; the shape is noted where
it stands.

Unchanged from the last paper: Q17-Q31, Q33-Q40, Q41-Q44
stand as they stood after Paper 008. Questions closed by
