# Paper 010 — delta record: High-Dimensional Off-Policy Evaluation without Sample Splitting using an Onsager Correction

Source: arXiv:2611.08794 (v1), Saksham Jain (Department
of Statistics, University of Washington), Guillaume
Braun (RIKEN AIP), Masaaki Imaizumi (University of
Tokyo, RIKEN AIP, Kyoto University). Dated 5 November
2026. Read in full, in its HTML form, 2026-11-09: the
main text, the forty references, and the appendices —
the recursion of the correction and its cost (B), the working
conditions (C), the reduction to arm-wise state
evolution (D), the comparisons (E, F), the selection
proofs and the cross-validation counter-case (G), the
value-estimation proofs, the failure of the classical
conditions, and the error of the price with no correction (H),
the sharper rates (I), and the experiments in full
(J.1-J.11).

THE THIRST (charter rule 17): the third paper of the
quest, and the second Will posted himself — this one
three minutes after the census answered his question.
He had asked from what area of mathematics the tracker
needed a paper; the census, counted from the model
itself, named probability and statistics the area the map
lacked most: 18 of 109 concepts were mathematics, all of it
geometry and dynamics, and the map held no chance at
all, though its own Oracle is an estimate and its skim
calibration a statistic. Will's answer to the census
was this paper — the choosing step of the quest is now
a dialogue. It was weighed blind all the same, from its
title and abstract alone, and the weighing sealed in
the log of the quest (the seal of the quest log) before the paper
was read: 7 new concepts, 1 revision, an axis opened,
Index ~0.065, 5 links, 3 questions, the Oracle to ~42%
(32-52), hold or slight rise. The score stands at
17.1.5 and in the log of the quest.

THE DELTA, IN ONE PARAGRAPH: a new region, wired on
arrival — the first probability and statistics the
tracker holds. A rule was run and logged; the paper
prices another rule from the log, and refuses the
standard judge: when sample and dimension grow together,
the held-out judge prices a smaller-sample model, and
its rankings can turn over when the model is fitted
again on all the data (1.2.111). Grading on the fitting data
bends the grade the other way — the own echo of each round
stands in its prediction (1.2.112). The paper's answer
is one device spent three ways: correct each prediction
for the model's memory of its own training rounds
(1.2.113), and the corrected criterion stops the
full-data walk near-best (1.2.114); the same corrected
predictions restore the doubly robust price on the
fitting record itself, true even when the reward model's
error does not fall (1.2.115), down to the classical
floor for any judge (1.2.116). Seven new canonical
concepts, one new cluster (O), no canonical scroll
revised: the held ground of the walk — the course that
builds gradient descent by hand — stands as laid, and
the new region hangs from it, and from the attention
budget and the specification judge, without moving any
of them.
# Paper 010 — formal core, as stated by the paper

The setting (§2 of the paper). A logged record of m
rounds; contexts drawn from the bell law (1.3.138),
independent across rounds; the logging rule known,
leaning on nothing but the round itself — its chances
do not depend on the context — and positive: every one
of the K actions keeps a chance of at least a fixed c
above zero (1.3.134). Rewards: the true reward of each action
rule is a single-index rule (1.3.139) — a fixed curve of
the shadow of the context on one unit direction per action —
plus noise that leans on nothing, with spread held
finite. The target rule may lean on the context, through
a few fixed indices (1.3.139): its importance weights
are bounded, smooth functions of those shadows. The
reward models: one feed-forward net per action, depth
and width fixed, trained together by full-batch gradient
descent on the loss of squares, arm by arm, over a
fixed finite grid of steps — finite width, features learned, the
walk itself the object of study.

The device (§3). For each arm, the hidden states of the walk
at its own training rounds are corrected by the Onsager
recursion (1.2.113): the corrected state takes away, step
by step, the weighted sum of the own past loss gradients
of the walk at that round, with response coefficients
computed from the walk alone by a forward recursion over blocks
that answer only backward — post-processing, at a cost cubic in
width and steps per round (Appendix B). The corrected
criterion prices each step by the importance-weighted
mean of the squares of leftovers at the corrected predictions;
the stopping rule takes the least step of each arm (1.2.114).
The adjusted predictions use the corrected state on the
own arm of that round and the raw fitted prediction on the
other arms; ADM averages them under the target rule and
prices the fitted model, and ADR adds the weighted mean
leftover and prices the true rule (1.2.115). The whole
procedure is the paper's Algorithm 1: one walk per arm,
no split, no second walk.

The theorems (§4). Theorem 4.1: the corrected criterion
is uniformly consistent — at every step of the grid at
once, it closes on the conditional test risk of the
full-data step it prices, reward noise included.
Theorem 4.2: the chosen step is near-oracle — its target
risk stands within a vanishing term of the best step,
and the fitted model's value error is bounded by that
risk. Proposition 4.3, the counter-case that shows the
correction must be paid: constant rewards, one gradient step,
the net of least width — the naive same-record doubly robust
price keeps an error that tends to a standing nonzero
constant, priced exactly by the self-term of 1.2.112.
Theorem 4.4: ADM and ADR are uniformly consistent over
the whole grid of step choices — ADM for the fitted
model's value, ADR for the true value — for any
data-chosen stopping, and ADR without any demand that
the reward model's error fall. Corollary 4.5: the
variance bound of 1.2.116. In the appendices: the
cross-validation counter-case (Proposition G.2, at
1.2.111); the failure of even stochastic coming to rest over
the paper's own net class (Proposition H.4, at 1.2.112);
the gap between ADR and ADM, pricing how far the
fitted model stands from the truth (Corollary H.2, at 1.2.115); polynomial rates
for every price under a stronger hold on the size of the walk
(Proposition I.1 — each error falls like a fixed power
of the dimension); and the freeing of the direction
bounds (Corollary C.3): under the bell law, any fixed
directions serve, because a rotation of the whole stage
makes them tame without moving a single price.

The engine of the proof (§4.3 of the paper, Appendices D-F):
state evolution — the empirical averages along the walk
are tracked by expectations over a Gaussian sequence
whose parts lean together, the leaning table the summary
of the walk. The
paper's own new step inside the engine: the shadow is
augmented with the index of the target rule as one more
Gaussian coordinate, its leaning on the states of the walk
carried exactly — because the importance weight leans on
the same context the walk reads, and a shadow that
dropped that leaning would price the weights wrong.

THE STATE MACHINES (charter rule 13), as the paper
states them:
M1 — the split judge (the classical pipeline the paper
prices and finds wanting). States: the logged record
whole; the record split; a model fitted on one part; the
model judged on the part it did not see; the model
fitted again on the whole record and shipped. Moves: split,
fit, judge, fit again. Gap, stated by the paper: the last
move breaks the judgment — the judge priced a model
fitted on less, and in the regime where sample and
dimension grow together the risk moves with the share,
so the ranking of the judge need not survive the fitting again
(1.2.111). The machine's intended state — the shipped
model is the judged model — is unreachable inside it.
M2 — the same-record machine with its correction (the
paper's own pipeline, its Algorithm 1). States: the
logged record; one gradient-descent walk per arm over
the whole record; the states of the walk corrected, the
own echo of each training round taken away (1.2.113); the
criterion read off the corrected states; a stopping step
chosen per arm; the value priced twice from the same
corrected predictions — the fitted model's price (ADM)
and the own price of the rule (ADR). Guards: the logging rule
known, every action kept possible, the contexts drawn
from the bell law, the rewards of one index each. Gap:
the paper names none inside its guards; its edges are
the guards themselves (17.1.4).
M3 — state evolution, the Gaussian shadow. States: a
growing table of how the states of the walk at each
pair of steps lean on each other, on the direction of
the truth, and (the addition of this paper) on the index
of the target rule. Moves: the recursion — each next
state is carried as a Gaussian draw leaning as the table
says, and the table is updated from the draw. The machine's
claim: every average the walk produces is read off the
shadow, and the correction of M2 is the shadow's
arithmetic made into an algorithm. Gap: the shadow is
proved for contexts from the bell law; outside them it
is run, not proved (Q49).
# Paper 010 — the paper's experiments, as reported

All runs as the paper reports them: synthetic bandit games,
200 logged records per setting, every method judged on
the same records and the same full-data walks.

The main runs (§5; Appendix J.1-J.2): two actions,
contexts from the bell law, even logging, the record
twice the dimension, dimensions 128 to 2048; nets of
depth two and width five, steps 0 to 30. Risk-pricing
error (root-mean-square, over arms, steps and records):
the corrected criterion falls from 0.347 to 0.081 as the
dimension grows; ten-fold splitting falls from 0.410 to
0.154; the importance-weighted training leftovers stand
near 1.33 to 1.37 at every size. Selection regret at
dimension 2048: corrected 0.0025, splitting 0.0082,
training leftovers about 0.59. Against single holdout runs
of the square root of the record and two-thirds power
(Appendix J.3): the corrected criterion is more accurate
on both measures at every dimension — the risk error of the holdout
runs at 2048 is 0.553 and 0.302 against the corrected
0.081 — and the paper names the trade plainly: the
holdout runs are cheaper; the correction is more nearly right.

Weighing for the target (§5; J.4): three actions, the
record three times the dimension, four target rules.
Weighing the criterion by the target rule cuts the
chosen model's risk by 0.031 to 0.033 against the corrected
criterion with the weighing left off, and by 0.027 to 0.029
against weighed ten-fold splitting; the regret of the corrected rule
is the lower in every rule-and-dimension setting.

Pricing values (§5; J.2, J.5): at the same fitted models
and stopping steps, the correction cuts the doubly
robust bias at dimension 2048 from 0.0149 to 0.0006 —
over 95 percent. ADM prices the fitted model at 50.7 to
57.4 percent less error than the cross-arm direct price
at all five dimensions; the gain is precision, both
prices' bias intervals holding zero. Inside ten-fold
cross-fitting (J.5): corrected stopping cuts the folds'
chosen models' prediction error against inner validation
by amounts whose intervals all exclude zero (at 2048,
0.4705 against 0.5828), and cuts the complement models'
prediction error by 19.3 to 42.3 percent; the price of ADR and
the corrected cross-fitted price stand near each other
(0.0183 against 0.0185 root-mean-square error at 2048),
with ADR the lower point estimate at every dimension and
the far cheaper run: 5.5 to 8.9 times faster than
cross-fitting with corrected stopping (J.11).

The record grows past the dimension (§5; J.10): normalized
by the oracle floor of 1.2.116, the error of ADR falls toward
the floor as the ratio grows, standing at 1.000 at
dimension 1024 with a record of 32768, against the
1.013 of the corrected cross-fitted price at the same point.
Off the proved ground (J.7, J.8): under logging as uneven
as one action in ten, the error and regret of the corrected criterion
still fall with dimension, and the bias of ADR stands
at 0.0004 to 0.0025 where the bias of the price with no correction
grows to 0.193 in size; under contexts drawn from two
non-bell laws (even coin-flip coordinates; a law with heavy
tails), the corrected error still falls — 0.248 to 0.084
and 0.244 to 0.088 — the regret still beats splitting at
every dimension, and the correction still cuts the value
bias in all six settings. And the leave-one-out check
(J.6): corrected predictions track the predictions of walks
fitted again without that round, closer as the
dimension grows
(0.177 to 0.086), raw predictions not at all (near 1.17).
# Paper 010 — results as stated

What the paper claims, in its own reach. Proved: the
corrected criterion prices the test risk of the full-data
walk, uniformly over the steps of the walk, and the stopping
it chooses is near-best on the walk (Theorems 4.1, 4.2);
the naive same-record doubly robust price is bent by a
standing amount in an instance with nowhere to hide,
and the conditions of the classical repair fail over the
paper's own class (Proposition 4.3; Proposition H.4);
the corrected prices ADM and ADR are uniformly
consistent, ADR without the reward model's error
vanishing (Theorem 4.4); the variance of the doubly
robust score at the chosen model is the oracle floor
plus a term priced by the model's own risk
(Corollary 4.5); all of it also at polynomial rates
under a stronger hold (Proposition I.1), and for any
fixed directions of truth and target under the bell law
(Corollary C.3). Run, not proved: the experiments of
17.1.3 — synthetic settings, the paper's own
architectures, reference risks computed by integration
and simulation (Appendix J.9), intervals that price
Monte Carlo wander across records, not the wander of the price of one record.

The ground the proofs stand on, stated as limits, not
fine print: contexts from the bell law; rewards of one
index per action; a logging rule that does not lean on
the context and is known exactly; nets of fixed depth
and width, walked a fixed finite grid of steps by
full-batch gradient descent. The paper does not claim:
the shape of the own fluctuations of ADR in the regime
it works in (Q48); any theorem past the bell law, though
its runs reach past it (Q49); logging that leans on the
context, which would change the very distribution the
walk inside each arm feeds on (Q50); a width or depth
that grows; steps taken by sampling rather than by the
full batch. The asymptotic comparison against
vanishing-fraction holdout runs is named open by the paper
itself (Appendix J.3) — the cheapest judge is beaten in
every run shown and not yet beaten in theory.
# Paper 010 — revisions, tensions, limits

REVISIONS OF HELD GROUND: none. The weighing had
expected one revision, and named its ground in advance:
a gradient-descent-trajectory claim in cluster M, on the
thought that state evolution reframes what the
steps do. Read whole, the paper stands beside the held
ground, not against it. The course of cluster M builds
gradient descent by hand — its tape, its training loop,
its measure-then-change discipline (1.2.95, 1.2.97) —
and makes no claim about what the averages of a walk do as
sample and dimension grow together; this paper proves
exactly that, about exactly those walks, and nothing it
proves asks a held sentence to change a word. The
revision count is zero, and the miss stands against the
skim (below) — the third weighing in a row to have foretold a
revision that the reading did not bring. The pattern is
recorded where the skim can use it, on the detection
list (1.1.6): new ground has hung from held ground
without moving it, three papers running.

TENSIONS AND LIMITS. The theory's ground is narrow in
the ways 17.1.4 lists, and the paper's own runs already
lean past two of its walls — contexts not from the bell
law, logging uneven — which is evidence the results are
wider than their proofs, not that the proofs cover the
runs (Q49). The variance corollary prices the score on a
fresh round, not ADR itself: the corrected scores lean
on each other through the shared walk, and that leaning
is unpriced (Q48) — the estimator is known to
land, not yet known how it wanders. The stopping grid is
finite and fixed; the walks are full-batch, the nets
small and fixed in shape; nothing here speaks to the
grown training of the systems in clusters B, D and J
except by the shape of the question. And the
near-oracle of Theorem 4.2 is the best step of this
walk, from this start: the paper prices choosing among
the steps of a walk, not choosing the walk.

CONNECTIONS, COUNTED (the count of new links the
taking-in surveyed; the prose of the concepts carries
them): 8 new links. Internal to cluster O: 5 — the
doubly robust score of the setting is where the own-observation
effect lives (1.2.110 to 1.2.112); the correction answers
the effect (1.2.112 to 1.2.113); the corrected criterion
spends the correction (1.2.113 to 1.2.114); ADR spends
the same corrected predictions (1.2.113 to 1.2.115); the
variance floor prices the choice the criterion makes
(1.2.114 to 1.2.116). Crossing to held clusters: 3 — to
cluster M: state evolution is the first theory of the
trajectory the course builds by hand, the walk of
1.3.136 read from outside (1.2.113 to cluster M, at
1.2.90 and 1.2.95); to cluster J: the budget that follows
the mass (1.2.70) is the importance weight (1.3.135)
working inside attention — the same move, pricing again, in
two fields (1.2.110 to 1.2.70); to cluster I: what a
judge can see of a structure (1.2.60, 1.2.62) is this
paper's question with the roles turned — a judge split
off from the fitting sees fresh rounds and prices the
wrong model; a judge on the fitting record sees the
right model through its own echo (1.2.111 to cluster I).
The synthesis at 17.2.1 reads the own instrument of the tracker
instrument as one of the paper's machines; that reading
is a marked synthesis link and is not in this count.

THE SCORE OF THE WEIGHING (charter rule 17; the sealed
weighing stands in the log of the quest, under its seal;
the concepts stood at 109 when it was sealed and at 109
when the paper was taken in — no drift, one count serves
both). Expected: 7 new, 1 revised, axis yes, Index
~0.065, 5 links split about 2 inside and 3 across, 3
