# Paper 010 — synthesis: the map gains the mathematics it runs on [synthesis]

[synthesis] The tracker has kept an estimate at its
center since its second day — the Research Oracle, a
number with a band — and a calibration since the Thirst
began: predictions sealed, scores kept, a running mean
of misses. Both are statistical instruments, and until
this paper the model that wields them held no statistics
to say what they are. Cluster O is that saying. Read
back through it, the Oracle is an estimator (1.3.133):
a rule that reads the stream and names a number for a
truth — the fixed point — it cannot see; the bands of the ledger
are its variance, kept by hand; and the Novelty
Index is a statistic whose own bias the tracker has been
chasing since Paper 005 (Q16): it prices size and not
distance, a bent grade of exactly the kind this cluster
exists to name.

[synthesis] M4 — the instrument of the tracker itself, read as
one of the paper's machines (rule 13, inferred; the
paper states its machines, this one belongs to the tracker
reading of itself). States: a skim fitted on the papers
already taken in; a new paper arriving by the stream;
the skim scored against the paper it never saw. Moves:
weigh blind and seal; read; score whole. The discipline of the quest
discipline is M1 rebuilt as a habit — the BEFORE record
is a fitted model, the AFTER record its held-out grade
— and this paper prices both what the discipline buys
(an honest judge, standing outside the fitting) and what
it cannot (the judge prices the skim at the size the
skim had when it weighed, and the model the score
describes has already grown by the paper being scored:
the problem of fitting again, wearing the own clothes of the tracker).
Whether the regime of the tracker is the proportional one —
papers against concepts, as samples against dimensions —
is the machine's open edge, and it is not a metaphor:
three times weighed, the counts stand at 10 papers
against 116 concepts, and the misses of the calibration are
falling as the ratio grows.

[synthesis] And the place of the region in the weave: cluster
M teaches the walk by building it; this paper shadows
the walk by a Gaussian law and reads its averages off
the shadow. Between the building and the shadow, the
walk itself — the single most-used machine in the
systems this tracker studies, the training of every
model in clusters B, D and J — now has both a floor and
a theory. The specification cluster asked what a judge
can see of a structure; this cluster answers for the
judge of models: it can see everything, if it corrects
for having been inside the fitting — and the correction
is computable from the walk alone. That is the paper's
deepest link to the work the tracker serves: a judge
that need not stand outside the thing it judges, priced
exactly. [synthesis]
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 010)

This paper opens a region and leaves its own edges
open, in its own words first:

Q48. The fluctuations of ADR. The corrected price is
consistent in the regime where sample and dimension grow
together, but the shape of its error there is unpriced:
the corrected scores lean on each other through the
shared walk, and that leaning is left open by the
paper itself. Is there a bell-law limit for ADR in this
regime — and does the classical limit, the record
growing past the dimension, recover the oracle floor as
the whole story, as the paper's runs suggest?
Q49. Past the bell law. Every theorem stands on contexts
drawn from the Gaussian law; the paper's runs reach past
it — two non-bell context laws, logging far from even —
and the corrected machinery still improves there. Is the
correction universal: does some form of it hold for any
context law with the moments tamed, or does the bell, in
being round, carry weight no run has yet priced?
Q50. Logging that leans on the context. The proofs hold
the logging rule flat — its chances ignore the context —
so inside each arm the contexts keep the law they were
drawn with. Real logging leans: which arm a round lands
in then bends the contexts the walk of that arm ever sees.
The paper names the extension and its price: new
dynamics, and a new correction. What does the correction
become when the own making of the record is inside the model?

Earlier questions this paper informs:
Q16 (the Index needs a connectivity term) — INFORMED, a
sixth time, and given its clearest run of points. Three
papers now stand at an Index of about 0.06: Paper 008,
landing inside a held cluster and revising held ground;
Paper 009, opening a field across one bridge; Paper 010,
opening a region wired three ways, on ground the model
itself stands on. Same size, three distances, three
different weights on the Oracle — the Index reads them
one number. The shape of the missing term has come into
focus each time a paper has informed it: not distance from the model entire, but
distance from the nearest held cluster, and — this paper
adds — whether the new ground is ground the model uses.
Q16 stands open; its term is nearly ready to name.

Unchanged from the last paper: Q17-Q31, Q33-Q40,
Q41-Q47 stand as they stood after Paper 009. Questions
closed by this paper: none.
