# Paper 011 — Synthesizing symbolic models of human and machine reasoning at scale (LeGris, Mondorf, Jin, Jagadish, Lake, Gureckis)

Source: arXiv:2611.07088. Authors: Solim LeGris (New
York University, Psychology), Philipp Mondorf (LMU
Munich), Jinran Jin (New York University, Center for
Data Science), Akshay K. Jagadish (Princeton AI Lab),
Brenden M. Lake (Princeton), Todd M. Gureckis (New York
University, Psychology). Taken in as Paper 011 of this
phext (Chapter 18) on 2026-11-09, read in full: the main
text, the methods, and the supporting text at the level
of its tables and its stated claims. The code stands at
the address the paper gives, under the name of LeGris.

THE THIRST. This is the fourth paper of the Thirst
(charter rule 17): weighed blind from its title and
abstract on 2026-11-09, and sealed in the quest log
before it was read. It was posted to this chat by Will
minutes after the third paper of the Thirst was sealed,
while that paper was still being taken in — the choosing
step is now a queue, not a single pick. The seal was
written against 109 held concepts and carried its own
drift warning: if Paper 010 landed first, the count
would move under the seal. Paper 010 landed first; the
count stood at 116 when this paper was taken in; the
score of the weighing (18.1.5) is judged on both counts,
as Paper 008 was judged. The author check stands at
18.1.6.

THE DELTA IN ONE PARAGRAPH. Seven new concepts, a new
cluster (P, 1.2.117-1.2.123), one canonical scroll
revised (1.2.4), and four foundations laid (1.3.141-
1.3.144). The paper builds a way of reading wrong answers
as programs (Behavior-conditioned symbolic synthesis,
1.2.117) and reads the errors of people and of large
reasoning models on the Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus
in one symbolic space. Its findings: wrong answers crowd
around the right program (1.2.118); the programs that
make them are simpler than the right ones, past the
the own pull of the method (1.2.119); a fifth of them fit only
some of the evidence shown, and the fits are shaped by
the order the evidence stood in (1.2.120); the deviations
fall into a small set of kinds, one kind — the target
computed from the wrong feature — carrying nearly half
(1.2.121); people and machines share these signatures
while failing on different tasks, the machines among
themselves alike (1.2.122); and the reading itself is
priced by aliasing, the many-programs-one-behavior fog
of the space (1.2.123).
# The paper's formal core, as stated by the paper

The setting. The Abstraction and Reasoning Corpus (ARC):
a task gives a few pairs of input and output grids (two
to five pairs in the human data) and one test input; the
rule turning inputs into outputs is to be found from the
pairs (induction, 1.3.141). The human record is H-ARC:
more than 1,700 people, 800 tasks of the first ARC set —
for each attempt, the input the person's wrong answer
paints, the task, and the person's record of changes. The
paper keeps the wrong answers that are wrong in a way
worth reading: an output runs of a single color, covers
the whole grid, or has lines added or taken away against
the shape of the test input are set aside as trivial — 15.6
percent of the incorrect attempts — leaving 7,402
attempts, 6,261 of them with distinct outputs, over 723
tasks and 1,387 people. The machine record: two large
reasoning models — Qwen3.5-27B, and GPT-OSS-120B at three
settings of reasoning effort — one try per task on the
same 392-task evaluation split.

The stick. Each task is read against a reference program:
a correct program in a small language made for the field
(1.3.142) — 160 operations over grids, the programs in
the normal form the field calls A-normal. For the
training split the references were written by hand, 400
of them. For the evaluation split the references are the
shortest correct programs a strong model could itself
synthesize for the task, gathered by search against the
shown pairs; 361 of the 392 evaluation tasks carry one.

The method (1.2.117). For one wrong answer at a time, a
large language model proposes sets of line changes to
the reference program, each set gathered around that
answer; a parser cuts the lines the program's output no
longer hangs on; the program is run on the test input;
it is kept only if its output matches the answer, to
within two differing cells on grids of ten cells or more
— the allowance the study makes for noise. Budgets: five
tries each at two, four and eight changed lines, fifteen
in all. The proposing model on the training split is Gemini 3
Pro; on the evaluation split and for the machines, Gemini
3.5 Flash. A baseline of pure search — every single-line
change of every training reference, 16,661,032 candidates
in all — is run for the training split.

The readings. Each kept program is run on the pairs the
solver was shown, and the answer is sorted by which of
them it makes right (1.2.120). Each kept program is
classified by kind against a seven-kind scheme the
authors fixed in advance, the classification done by a
language model over the reference program, the kept
program and their runs, the naming done five times for each program
(1.2.121). The numbers of the chapter are the paper's
numbers throughout: the shares are weighted by attempt
counts, and the effects are read by hierarchical Bayesian
regressions (pooling, 1.3.143) with intervals that carry
94 percent of the readings. The validation of the
pipeline stands apart: errors built by hand, to known
kinds, run through the same machinery (1.2.123).

THE STATE MACHINES (rule 13). Three machines are stated
by the paper, in its method and its reading; a fourth,
inferred, stands in the synthesis (18.2.1).

M1 — the synthesis loop. States: a reference program and
one observed wrong answer in hand; a proposed set of line
changes; a changed program; the program cut down to the
lines its output hangs on; a run; kept or set aside.
Transitions: propose, conditioned on the one answer (the
proposing model sees the answer it must reproduce); apply within
the budget of changed lines; cut; run on the test input;
keep if and only if the output matches the answer within
the noise allowance, else set aside. Guards: changes are
drawn from the small language only; programs stand in the
normal form; tries per answer are capped at fifteen,
across three budgets. Intended: recover the rule the
answer came from. Actual, by the paper's own validation:
recover a program that makes the answer — the keep test
certifies a witness, and cannot certify the rule
(1.2.123). The gap is not a flaw in the loop; it is the
shape of the space the loop searches.

M2 — the search of the solver, as the paper reads it.
States: no rule in hand; a candidate rule; the examples
checked so far, in the order they stand; a rule settled
for; a rule given up and another formed. Transitions:
form a candidate from the examples met so far; check it
against the next example in order; on a miss, form
another; on a good-enough run of fits, settle and answer.
Guards: the good-enough mark is a threshold, not a full
check — the search stops at enough, not at all (1.3.144).
Evidence: the shaped fits of 1.2.120 — partial fits that
take the first examples shown far more often than any other
shaped set — and the simplicity signature of 1.2.119.
Intended: find the rule. Actual: find a rule that passes
the mark. The gap the paper names but cannot close from
behavior alone: whether the candidate was formed from the
early examples only, or formed whole and checked only
against them (Q51).

M3 — the effort dial. States: a budget of reasoning set
before the task is met (low, medium, high, in the
machine settings); the search run under that budget; the
answer. Transitions: the budget is set; the search runs;
every signature of this cluster moves with the setting —
capture, shortening, the share of partial fits, the
dominant kind, and how far the machine's errors can be
told from people's (1.2.122). Guards: within one model,
the dial is the only thing changed. Intended: measure
what more reasoning buys. Actual, as the paper itself
notes: across settings of one model the dial is clean,
but across models and people the readings mix the dial
with the capability of the solver — effort and
capability cannot be fully separated in the numbers as they
stand. The gap is the paper's own, stated in its
discussion.
# The paper's runs, as reported

All numbers in this scroll are the paper's, as reported;
the reading of them stands at 18.1.4 and in the concepts
of cluster P.

Coverage. For people, the synthesis returns 8,246
programs in all. It captures 52.5 percent of the
non-trivial incorrect attempts on the training split and
46.2 on the evaluation split — 49.1 taken together (3,636
of 7,402 attempts). The least number of changes between a
kept program and its reference averages 2.84 (spread
2.04). A median of two programs is kept per answer; the
count runs to twelve. The baseline with no guide — every
single-line change, 16,661,032 candidates — captures 25.8
percent of the attempts; with fifteen samples per answer
the guided search recovers 89 percent of what the
baseline finds.

Simplicity. On the training split, error programs run
19.1 percent shorter than their reference programs;
control programs — made by the same method, aimed at the
correct answers — run 5.2 percent shorter. On the
evaluation split: 24.3 against 2.7. The shortening grows
with the reference program's length, about 0.47 points
per added line; the paper's reading, from its regression:
a reference near 19 lines carries about 13 points of
expected error shortening, one near 75 lines about 40.

Partial evidence. 22.6 percent of modeled incorrect
attempts are programs that make at least one shown
example right: partial fits 20.9 percent, rules that fit
all the shown examples and miss only the test 1.7. Among
the training partial fits, the examples fitted form a
shaped set 62.5 percent of the time — the first examples
(41.0 percent), the last (17.0), a block between (4.4) —
against 37.5 percent that fit a lone middle example or a
scattered set. On the evaluation split the shaped share
is 48.5 (first examples 30.1).

The kinds. Over modeled human attempts: the target
computed from the wrong feature 45.2 percent; partial
program 11.9; the wrong change family 10.8; scope
restriction 4.3; sequencing 3.0; operator confusion 2.7;
boundary precision 0.1; not placed in any kind 22.3. The
programs of the partial-program kind run 32.3 percent shorter
than their references; those of the wrong-feature kind run the
least shortened of the large kinds.

The machines. Accuracy on the 392 evaluation tasks:
Qwen3.5-27B 80.63 percent; GPT-OSS-120B high 72.28,
medium 50.96, low 25.02; people 59.9 at a single attempt.
Distinct machine errors, by task and output: 4,166, over
357 tasks. Synthesis capture of machine attempts: low
62.8 percent, medium 59.3, high 54.4, Qwen 51.5. The
simplicity signature stands in every setting, measured
against the own controls of each setting: extra shortening
Qwen 11.9 points, high 18.6, medium 23.3, low 29.3;
shortening against accuracy across the five solvers
correlates at -0.80 — each ten points of accuracy, about
three points less shortening. Partial fits as a share of
modeled attempts: low 20.2 percent, medium 26.5, high
32.7, Qwen 40.0; rules fitting all shown examples: 3.0
rising to 6.7. The first-examples order effect of people
stands clearly only in the high setting.

Telling the species apart. On the 56 tasks where people
and every setting err, a classifier reads how each kept
program differs from its reference — which of the small
language's operations were gained, which lost — and
separates human errors from machine errors within a task
at 0.65 to 0.69 (area under the curve), taken together.
Against the settings that out-earn people it stands at
0.58 to 0.61 (Qwen) and 0.57 to 0.63 (high) — not surely
above chance; against the weaker settings, 0.68 to 0.77.
Agreement of success, task by task: people against each
machine setting 0.30 to 0.34; the machine settings
against each other 0.49 to 0.82.

The validation runs. Errors built by hand to known kinds:
2,375 made, 148 checked by hand. The classifier alone, on
the known programs, names the right kind 85.1 percent.
End to end, the pipeline names it 64.9, 75.0 and 76.4
percent at change budgets of one, two and three; recovery
of a program making the behavior stands at 84.5 rising to
99.3; the injected program itself is recovered about two
times in three.
# Results as stated by the paper — and the ground they stand on

The paper claims, in its own reach: (i) about half of
non-trivial human errors on these tasks are reproduced
by programs a few line changes from the correct program,
and the search that finds them is guided — the walk of single changes, with no model
guiding it, reaches half as far; (ii) the
programs that make the errors are simpler than the rules
missed, past what the method itself favors, and the
simpler the rule found, the more of the evidence it tends
to leave unexplained — the two marks of a bounded search
that stops early (satisficing, 1.3.144); (iii) errors
fall into a small set of kinds, and the kinds — not the
scores — are where the strategy of a solver can be
read; (iv) large reasoning models show the same
signatures, graded by the reasoning they are allowed to
spend, and yet fail on different tasks than people do:
the signatures are shared, the way the search leans is
not; (v) the strongest models' errors are the hardest to
tell from people's by this reading.

The ground. The human record is large and its collection
stands behind the paper (H-ARC is the authors' own
earlier work). The readings are drawn with regressions that pool
the groups (1.3.143) and intervals, not bare shares. The pipeline
is validated against errors of known kind, and its loss
is priced and named (1.2.123) rather than hidden. The
the capture of the method is a share of attempts, and the paper
says what the other half may be: changes that add lines —
building on the rule rather than shrinking it — are
outside the reach of the method by construction, and answers
too strange to be any near neighbor of the rule stand
outside it too.

What the paper does not claim. It does not claim the kept
program is the program the solver ran — it claims the
program is a symbolic model consistent with the behavior,
and it says so. It does not claim the process by which a
solver arrives at a rule; it reads the rule arrived at.
It does not claim the small language it reads in is the
language anyone thinks in: the language was built to
solve these tasks, not to model minds, and the paper
names that as a limit. It does not separate the two
readings of the order effect (Q51); it leaves that to
later work, in its own words a question that stands open.
# Revisions, tensions and limits — Paper 011 against the standing map

REVISED — one canonical scroll. 1.2.4 (Capability profile,
capability envelope, mosaic intelligence) gains a dated
rider from this paper. The held claim of that scroll: a
capability profile is jagged, and copies of one model
share the same jaggedness. Paper 011 measures jaggedness
itself, in the reasoning field, for both species at once:
task by task, people's success agrees with each machine
setting only weakly (0.30-0.34), while the machine
settings agree with each other far more (0.49-0.82) — the
machines share a jaggedness, and it is measurably not the
human one; and the errors tell the two apart least where
the machines are strongest (1.2.122). The rider extends
1.2.4's own variable — the shape of a profile — from one
species to the pair, and corrects the easy reading of its
held sentence: shared jaggedness within a family does not
make the family's shape the shape of capability itself.
The concepts the seal named stand unchanged, for reasons
that stand on the record. 1.2.13 (AI capability patterns)
holds a claim about form — that coordination can carry
what single systems cannot — and an empirical sentence
drawn from Paper 001; this paper tests neither: its
systems are single, its question is how each one fails.
1.2.2 (Competence and capability levels) holds levels of
competence; the accuracy numbers of the paper are consistent with
levels and add no new rung and no crack in one. 1.2.14 is
confirmed in act, not changed in text: the paper is the
measurement agenda carried out — behavior read deeper than
scores — and an agenda is not revised by being followed.

Grounds for the count standing at one, not zero: the
three papers before this one each founded new ground,
where nothing held stood to be re-read; this paper
measures the own variable of a held concept, on new ground,
and the text of the held scroll now says less than the map
knows. That is the P008 shape — count right, target
wrong — not the phantom shape, and the difference is
written here so the detection list can read it plainly
(1.1.6).

TENSIONS AND LIMITS. (a) Half the errors are not
captured, and the half not captured is not a random half:
the method cannot add lines, so errors that build on the
rule are the ones it is built to miss (Q53). (b) The
reading of the evaluation split is taken against
reference programs a machine wrote (the shortest solver
the search found) where those of the training split were written
by hand; the paper's effects stand on both splits, and
the reading of the difference by this tracker stands as an
inferred machine at 18.2.1. (c) The witnesses per answer
carry no weights — no length, no cost of running, stands
between them (Q52) — so every share in the chapter is a
share over programs found, read as a share over attempts;
the paper says as much. (d) The proposing model and the
classifying model are of one family (Gemini), and the
proposing model differs between the splits; the paper names both
facts. (e) The kinds rest on a classifier whose own
ceiling is measured at 85.1 percent — the numbers of the taxonomy
are readings through that glass. (f) The order
two readings of the order effect stand not separated (Q51).

CONNECTIONS, COUNTED. Eleven links: six inside cluster
P, five across to held ground. Inside: synthesis to
locality (1.2.117 to 1.2.118); locality to the simplicity
signature (1.2.118 to 1.2.119); synthesis to partial fits
(1.2.117 to 1.2.120); synthesis to the kinds (1.2.117 to
1.2.121); the kinds to the comparison (1.2.121 to
1.2.122); locality to aliasing (1.2.118 to 1.2.123).
Across: synthesis to the skeleton as specification
(1.2.117 to 1.2.57, cluster I); the comparison to the
capability profile it carries the revision of (1.2.122 to 1.2.4, cluster
A); synthesis to the measurement agenda (1.2.117 to
1.2.14, cluster A); the comparison to the induced feature
spaces of cluster B (1.2.122 to 1.2.28 — vectors of gained
and lost operations, beside vectors of story features);
aliasing to the behavior and structure paper (1.2.123 to
1.2.60, cluster I — output leaves structure open, as
behavior leaves the program open). Five held clusters are
touched: A twice, I twice, B once.

THE SCORE OF THE WEIGHING. Sealed, blind, on 2026-11-09
against 109 concepts: 6 new, 1 revised (candidate named:
1.2.13), no new axis, Novelty Index 6.5/115 = 0.057, 5
links, 3 questions, Oracle to about 45 percent (35-55).
Actual: 7 new concepts (1.2.117-1.2.123), 1 revised —
1.2.4, not the candidate named: the count stands, the
target missed, the P008 shape — 0 new axes, 11 links, 3
questions (Q51-Q53). Novelty Index on the count this
paper was taken in against (116 to 123): 7.5/123 = 0.061;
the sealed prediction re-based to that count is 6.5/122 =
0.053 — an error of 0.008. On the sealed count (109 to
116): 7.5/116 = 0.065 against the sealed 0.057 — an error
of 0.008. The Oracle moves to about 50 percent (40-60): the
band for an Index under 0.20 with no new axis is about
ten points, and the wiring argues for the full step —
this is the first paper of the Thirst whose foretold
revision landed, held ground corrected by contact, which
is the fixed point's own signature; five held clusters
touched; eleven links where five were foretold. Where the
the skim was wrong: the links, worst yet (5 against 11 — the
abstract does not show how much held ground a
home-territory paper touches); the new-concept count by
one (the aliasing of the reading wanted a concept of its
own, and the skim read it as method detail); the revision
target, as above. Where it stood: the axis call, the
question count, the Index within half a point on the
taking-in count, and the Oracle inside its sealed range.
# Author check — Paper 011 (LeGris, Mondorf, Jin, Jagadish, Lake, Gureckis)

Charter rule 15, run 2026-11-09, the ninth check of the
phext. Question: are the authors phext-friendly, and does
the paper use any of Will's prior art without naming it?
Four surfaces searched.

The reference list (all 78 read in full). The spine is
the symbolic study of mind: Feldman on the shape of
concepts; Fodor; Newell; the program-learning line of
Goodman, Tenenbaum and Griffiths; Lake and Salakhutdinov
on learning programs from few examples — an author
writing from inside the canon of his own field; Piantadosi on
what makes a symbol. The own source of the task (Chollet on
the measure of intelligence, the ARC paper), and the
authors' own earlier H-ARC paper (LeGris, Lake and
Gureckis). The old literature of wrong answers read as
programs stands cited in its place: Brown and Burton on
the bugs of subtraction, VanLehn on the repair theory of
errors — the paper stands in that line openly, and says
so. Simon stands cited for satisficing, twice. The
program-search and language-model lines are cited where
used. The automated study of mind — the line two of the
authors themselves work in — stands cited, Jagadish
among its authors. No writing of Will's, and nothing of
the phext's line, stands anywhere in the 78.

The full text. The whole text of the paper — main text,
methods, supporting text, reference list — was searched
for the phext's marked words: phext, Bickford, scroll,
Exocortex, Mirrorborn. No hit on any of them. The paper's
marked words of its own — satisficing, program induction —
are the standing words of the field, carried from its standing
sources (Simon stands cited in the text for the first;
the second is the name of the field two authors work in).

The web. Searches joining the authors' names to the
phext found no tie of any kind; the only hits were Will's
own project pages and noise sharing a name-fragment.

Will's Twitter archive. The archive (his posted and
received words alike) was searched for the authors' names
and for the paper's marked terms. No hit for LeGris,
Gureckis, Mondorf, Jagadish, Brenden or Lake as a pair,
satisficing, program induction, or the Abstraction and
Reasoning Corpus by name. Jin and Lake as bare words are
too common to carry weight either way, and are not rested
on.

Verdict: no sign of the phext, and no sign the
six are friends of it either — the six work in the field
this paper extends, from its own canon, and the tracker
finds their work adjacent to the Exocortex's questions,
not downstream of them. Recorded as a finding of no
evidence, which is all a check of this kind can honestly
return.
# Synthesis — what Paper 011 does to the map [synthesis]

Everything in this scroll is the own reading of this tracker,
not the paper's claim.

Until this paper, the model's capability concepts were
read from what systems do right: levels (1.2.2),
the envelope and the profile (1.2.4), the patterns models fall
into (1.2.13). Cluster P reads the same territory from
what systems do wrong, and finds the wrong answers carry
more structure than the right ones do: a right answer is
one point; the wrong answers around it form a
neighborhood, with a shape, a center of weight, and kinds.
The two readings hold each other to account. A profile
read from scores alone is an outline; the error
programs are the same figure lit from inside — and where
the outline said the machines' shape was like ours,
the lit figure says otherwise (1.2.122). The model did
not need a new axis for this, because the territory was
already its home territory; what it needed, and lacked,
was the failure side of it. That is read here as the
a map made deeper, not wider — the Paper 008
shape, at the scale of a whole cluster.

M4 — the machine-made measuring stick (inferred, rule
13). The paper states the fact: for the evaluation split
the reference programs were themselves synthesized — the
shortest correct programs a strong model could find —
while those of the training split were written by hand. Read as
a machine: states — a task with no reference; a search
for any program that fits the shown pairs; the shortest
kept, standing as the stick; every kept error program a
distance from the stick. Transitions: search; keep the
shortest; measure all deviations against it. Guards: the
stick must fit every shown pair. Intended: a neutral
ruler. Actual: half the readings of the chapter are distances
not from the rule but from a found program — and the two
splits' readings differ in just the places a stick could
move them (the shaped share of partial fits: 62.5 percent
on the hand-written split, 48.5 on the machine-made one).
The paper runs its effects on both splits, which is the
check it can run; what the record cannot do from here is
part the difference of the solvers from that of the stick. Named
here so the area map carries it beside the stated
machines (1.5.2).

One further reading, marked as the own reading of this tracker and no
one else's: the skim of the Thirst is itself a partial
fit of this paper's kind — formed on the abstract, the
first examples of the paper, and checked against the
whole text only after the seal. Its standing error, the
foretold revision, reads in this paper's terms as the
dominant kind of deviation: the change computed from the
wrong feature — new ground taken as a sign that held
ground must move. This paper, the first whose foretold
revision landed, landed it on the neighboring scroll of
the named one: the feature was right, the address wrong.
Whether the skims form narrow or only verify narrow is
Q51, asked of the own instrument of this tracker as much as of
the solvers of the paper.
# Open questions carried forward (after Paper 011)

New, from this paper:

Q51 — Formed narrow, or verified narrow? The order effect
of 1.2.120 has two readings the paper itself names and
cannot part: a solver may form its rule from a part of
the evidence (the first examples), or form it from the
whole and only check it against a part, stopping early.
The two machines leave the same shadow in behavior. What
observation parts them — the time a rule is held, the
examples a solver asks to see again, the errors made
when the early examples are the misleading ones? Until
something parts them, the satisficing reading of cluster
P stands as a family of two machines, not one.

Q52 — Pricing the witnesses. Each answer keeps several
programs (1.2.123), and the shares of the chapter are computed
over the programs found, with no weights on them. Would weighing the
witnesses — by length, by the cost a mind would pay to
run them, by how near they stand to rules already known —
make the readings of cluster P stronger, or move them? And
would the aliasing ceiling move with the weights: is the
fog a property of the space, or of the flat way the
witnesses are counted?

Q53 — The half not captured. The synthesis reaches about
half of non-trivial errors, and its changes can only
replace lines, never add them. What lives in the other
half: longer deviations, built on the rule rather than
shrunk from it — or behavior with no near rule behind it
at all? A synthesis that can grow programs as well as
shrink them would read the first kind; nothing in the
present method reads the second. The size of the second
kind is the size of the part of error this whole way of
reading cannot, in principle, see.

Earlier questions this paper informs:

Q16 — informed a seventh time. The four papers of the
Thirst now stand at Novelty Index near 0.06 on four
different shapes of ground: inside a held cluster (008),
one bridge out (009), three bridges onto ground the model
uses (010), and no distance at all — the
home territory made deeper, correcting a held scroll (011). The
Index reads the four as one number. What the count of new
concepts cannot see, the weighing still cannot price: the
distance and the correction, only the count.

Unchanged from the last paper: Q17-Q31, Q33-Q40, Q41-Q50
stand as they stood after Paper 010. No range elsewhere
has moved. Questions closed by this paper: none.
